var e={"tech:machinecode":{intro:`Stored-program coding means writing a computer's instructions as numbers and placing them in the machine's own memory, right beside the data they work on. The computer fetches each instruction in turn, decodes it and carries it out. Because the program is just numbers in memory, it can be changed in minutes instead of by rewiring cables and switches.`,how:`Each instruction is a binary word: some bits say what to do, such as load, subtract, store or jump if negative, and the rest give a memory address. Programmers worked out the bits on paper and entered them one word at a time with switches or punched tape. A single wrong bit could send the machine wandering through memory, so checking a program by hand took longer than writing it.`,history:`In 1945 John von Neumann's 'First Draft of a Report on the EDVAC' described a computer that kept its program in the same memory as its data. On 21 June 1948 the Manchester Baby, built by Freddie Williams, Tom Kilburn and Geoff Tootill, became the first machine to run a program held in its own memory. Its first program of 1948, written by Kilburn, hunted for the highest proper factor of 2 to the power 18 by trying every number in turn. In May 1949 EDSAC at Cambridge became the first stored-program computer to offer a regular computing service to scientists. In February 1951 the Ferranti Mark 1, a production version of the Manchester design, was delivered to Manchester University as the first such computer sold commercially.`,facts:[`The Baby's whole memory was 32 words of 32 bits, held as dots on a cathode-ray tube (1948).`,`The Baby was about 5 metres long and weighed nearly a tonne (1948).`,`EDSAC's first program, run on 6 May 1949, printed a table of squares.`]},"tech:assembler":{intro:`An assembler lets a programmer write instructions as short memorable words and labels instead of binary numbers. A small program, itself stored in the computer, reads those words and turns each one into the matching machine instruction. It is one of the first pieces of software written to help people write software.`,how:`Each line holds one instruction: a mnemonic such as ADD or JMP, and the name or number of a memory cell. The assembler looks the mnemonic up in a table, works out the addresses and places the binary words in memory. With symbolic labels a programmer can insert a line without renumbering every jump that follows it. One line of assembly still makes exactly one machine instruction, so the programmer keeps full control.`,history:`In 1947 Kathleen Booth, working with Andrew Booth at Birkbeck College in London, devised a 'contracted notation' for writing programs for their ARC computer. In May 1949 David Wheeler's 'initial orders' on Cambridge's EDSAC read instructions punched as a letter and a decimal address and converted them into binary as they loaded from tape. In the mid-1950s IBM's SOAP for the IBM 650 went further, placing each instruction around the spinning drum memory so it arrived under the read head just when needed. In 1956 Roy Nutt of United Aircraft wrote SAP for the IBM 704, and the SHARE user group passed it to 704 sites across the country.`,facts:[`The IBM 704, home of SAP, could execute about 40,000 instructions a second (1956).`,`EDSAC's initial orders let programmers write A for add and S for subtract in 1949.`]},"tech:subroutines":{intro:`A subroutine is a piece of program written once and used again whenever it is needed, such as one that works out a square root or prints a number. A library is a shelf of such tested pieces that every programmer at a computer centre can borrow. Programs get shorter, quicker to write and less likely to contain mistakes.`,how:`The main program jumps into the subroutine and leaves a note of where it came from, so the subroutine can jump back when it has finished. Library routines were punched on paper tape and kept in a cabinet beside the machine. A programmer copied the tapes needed onto the end of a new program, and the loader adjusted their addresses to wherever they landed in memory, so the same tape worked in any program.`,history:`In 1949 the EDSAC team at Cambridge began building a library of tested routines on paper tape for everyone who used the machine. In 1949 David Wheeler devised a neat way of calling a routine and returning from it that became known as the Wheeler jump. In 1951 Maurice Wilkes, David Wheeler and Stanley Gill published The Preparation of Programs for an Electronic Digital Computer, which printed the library's routines so other centres could copy them. In 1952 Wheeler's short paper 'The use of sub-routines in programmes' set out the idea for American programmers at an ACM meeting in Pittsburgh.`,facts:[`David Wheeler received one of the world's first doctorates in computer science, from Cambridge, in 1951.`,`Maurice Wilkes won the Turing Award in 1967, the second person to receive it.`]},"tech:infotheory":{intro:`Information theory measures how much a message really says, in units called bits. A message you could have guessed carries little information; a surprising one carries a lot. The theory sets hard limits on how far any data can be squeezed, and on how fast it can be sent over a noisy line without errors.`,how:`Shannon treated a source of messages as a game of chance and defined its entropy: the average number of yes-or-no questions needed to pin down each symbol. English text has a lot of predictable structure, so it needs far fewer bits per letter than its alphabet suggests. A second result showed that every channel has a capacity: below it, clever coding can make errors as rare as you like; above it, nothing can.`,history:`In 1948 Claude Shannon of Bell Labs published 'A Mathematical Theory of Communication' in two parts in the Bell System Technical Journal. The 1948 paper was the first in print to use the word 'bit', short for binary digit, a name suggested by his colleague John Tukey. In 1949 the paper came out as a book with an introduction by Warren Weaver, carrying the ideas far beyond telephone engineers. In 1950 Shannon's Bell Labs colleague Richard Hamming published the first codes able to find and fix errors automatically. In 1951 Shannon had people guess the next letters of English text and estimated that each letter carries only about one bit.`,facts:[`Shannon's 1951 tests put English at roughly one bit per letter, against nearly five for random letters.`,`Hamming's 1950 code protected 4 data bits with 3 check bits and fixed any single error.`]},"tech:huffman":{intro:`Huffman coding gives each symbol a string of bits whose length depends on how often it appears: frequent symbols get short codes and rare ones long codes. No code is the start of another, so a stream of bits can be read back without gaps or separators. For a given set of symbol frequencies, no code that works symbol by symbol can do better.`,how:`Count how often each symbol occurs. Take the two rarest, join them under a new node whose count is their sum, and repeat until a single tree remains. Reading the path from the root to each leaf, left as 0 and right as 1, gives that symbol's code. Building from the rarest symbols upwards is exactly what makes the result optimal.`,history:`In 1951 David Huffman, a graduate student at MIT, was offered a choice by his teacher Robert Fano: sit the final exam, or write a term paper on finding the most efficient binary code. Shannon in 1948 and Fano in 1949 had built codes from the top down, splitting symbols into two equally likely halves, which came close but was not always optimal. In 1951 Huffman, about to give up and start revising, hit on building the tree from the bottom up instead. In 1952 he published the method as 'A Method for the Construction of Minimum-Redundancy Codes'. In 1980 the Group 3 fax standard used a modified Huffman code to send pages over telephone lines.`,facts:[`Huffman was about 25 years old when he found his method in 1951.`,`His 1952 paper filled just four pages of the Proceedings of the IRE.`]},"tech:compiler":{intro:`A compiler is a program that translates instructions written in a form convenient for people into the machine code a computer runs. The programmer works at a higher level, and the machine does the tedious work of choosing addresses, registers and jumps. Many experts doubted that code written by a machine could ever be as good as a skilled human's.`,how:`The earliest compilers worked like clever librarians: the programmer listed routines by code number, and the compiler fetched each from a tape library, fixed its addresses and stitched them into one program. Others read simple algebraic statements and translated each into a few machine instructions. Either way the translation was done once, before the program ran, rather than every time it ran.`,history:`In 1952 Grace Hopper's team at Remington Rand wrote the A-0 system for the UNIVAC I, which gathered numbered subroutines from magnetic tape into a complete program. Also in 1952 Alick Glennie at Manchester wrote Autocode for the Ferranti Mark 1, often called the first compiled programming language. In 1953 Hopper's team followed with A-2, which was given to customers who sent back their improvements. In 1954 Laning and Zierler's system for MIT's Whirlwind computer translated algebraic formulas straight into machine code, a demonstration that impressed IBM's John Backus.`,facts:[`The UNIVAC I that ran A-0 had 1,000 words of mercury delay-line memory (1952).`,`Hopper's A-2 of 1953 was one of the first programs shared with users along with its source.`]},"tech:usergroups":{intro:`A user group is a club of organisations that own the same model of computer and agree to pool their programs, tricks and complaints. Programmers were scarce and every site was writing the same routines, so sharing saved years of work. The group also spoke to the manufacturer with one loud voice about what it needed next.`,how:`Members kept a shared library of programs with standard write-ups, numbered and copied onto cards or tape for anyone who asked. They agreed common rules, such as one assembler and one way of calling subroutines, so that code from one site would run at another. Meetings were held a few times a year, with written minutes and formal requests sent to the maker.`,history:`In August 1955 people from firms running IBM 701 and 704 computers in the Los Angeles area, among them aircraft makers and the RAND Corporation, met and founded SHARE. In 1956 its members settled on one assembler, SAP, and one set of coding conventions, so a routine written at one company could run at another. In 1959 SHARE members finished SOS, the SHARE Operating System, for the new IBM 709. In 1961 owners of Digital Equipment Corporation's machines formed DECUS, the Digital Equipment Computer Users' Society, on the same model.`,facts:[`By its first anniversary in 1956, SHARE had about 60 member organisations.`,`SHARE marked 65 years in 2020, the oldest computer user group still running.`]},"tech:batchos":{intro:`A batch operating system is a resident monitor program that runs one job after another with no person stepping in between. Users hand in decks of punched cards, operators gather them onto tape, and the monitor reads each job, runs it, prints the results and moves straight on. The costly computer no longer sits idle while someone loads the next program.`,how:`Each job begins with control cards that tell the monitor what to do: compile this, load that, run it with these data. The monitor stays in a corner of memory and takes back control when a job ends, crashes or runs over its time. A cheaper machine often copied card decks onto magnetic tape and printed the output tapes, so the big machine only ever talked to fast tape drives.`,history:`In the early 1950s programmers booked a computer by the hour and set up their own jobs, leaving the machine idle for much of each session. In 1956 Robert Patrick of General Motors Research and Owen Mock of North American Aviation built GM-NAA I/O for the IBM 704, often counted as the first operating system. In 1959 SHARE members released SOS for the IBM 709, adding a standard way to assemble, load and debug jobs. By 1962 IBM supplied IBSYS for its 7090 and 7094, a monitor that tied FORTRAN, the assembler and other tools together, with small IBM 1401 machines feeding it tapes.`,facts:[`An IBM 7090 sold for about $2.9 million or rented for about $63,500 a month (1959).`,`IBM's transistorised 7090 of 1959 ran about six times faster than the 709 it replaced.`]},"tech:fortran":{intro:`FORTRAN, short for Formula Translation, lets scientists and engineers write calculations much as they would on paper, such as X = A*B + C, with loops and arrays built in. Its compiler turns those statements into machine code nearly as fast as an expert's. Programming no longer needs a specialist sitting between the scientist and the computer.`,how:`A program is a deck of punched cards, one statement per card, with columns kept for labels and continuation marks. DO loops repeat work over arrays, and an IF statement jumps to one of three labels depending on whether a value is negative, zero or positive. The compiler spent most of its effort optimising loops and the use of index registers, because its makers knew nobody would trust it if its code ran slowly.`,history:`In 1953 John Backus proposed to his managers at IBM a language for the 704, arguing that programming and debugging cost as much as running the machine. In 1954 his small team in New York issued a preliminary report describing the language. In April 1957 the first compiler reached 704 customers, and engineers found its code close to hand-written quality. In 1958 FORTRAN II added subroutines that could be compiled separately and joined later. By 1963 more than 40 FORTRAN compilers existed for computers from many makers. In 1966 FORTRAN 66 became the first programming language to get an official American standard.`,facts:[`By 1963 more than 40 FORTRAN compilers existed for different computers.`,`John Backus won the Turing Award in 1977, largely for FORTRAN.`]},"tech:lisp":{intro:`LISP, short for List Processor, is a language built around lists of symbols, and its programs are themselves written as lists. That makes it easy for a program to build, inspect and run other programs, which suits research into artificial intelligence. Memory is managed automatically: storage that nothing points to any more is found and reused without the programmer's help.`,how:`Everything is written in brackets with the operation first, as in (PLUS 2 3). Lists are chains of two-part cells; the names for the halves, CAR and CDR, came from parts of an IBM 704 machine word. When free cells run out, a garbage collector follows every pointer from the program's variables, marks each cell it reaches, and sweeps the unmarked ones back onto the free list.`,history:`In 1958 John McCarthy at MIT began designing LISP for artificial intelligence, a field he had named in 1955 in his proposal for a summer workshop at Dartmouth. Around 1959 his student Steve Russell saw that McCarthy's eval function, meant as theory, could be coded by hand, and so produced the first LISP interpreter on an IBM 704. In April 1960 McCarthy described the language in Communications of the ACM, including the first account of garbage collection. In 1962 Tim Hart and Mike Levin wrote a LISP compiler in LISP itself. In 1962 the LISP 1.5 Programmer's Manual spread the language to other universities.`,facts:[`McCarthy showed in 1960 that a whole language could rest on about seven basic operations.`,`Hart and Levin's 1962 compiler was among the first compilers written in its own language.`,`John McCarthy received the Turing Award in 1971.`]},"tech:algol":{intro:`ALGOL, the Algorithmic Language, was designed by an international committee of European and American scientists as a common, machine-independent way to write and publish algorithms. It brought nested blocks with their own local variables, procedures that can call themselves, and clean if-then-else statements. Its grammar was written in a precise notation, so anyone could tell exactly which programs were legal.`,how:`The grammar notation defines a language with rules such as: a number is a digit, or a number followed by a digit. From such rules a compiler writer knows every legal program without guessing. Programs are built of begin and end blocks; each block has its own variables, kept on a stack, which makes recursion natural. The language had no input or output statements, leaving those to each machine.`,history:`In 1958 American and European delegates, among them John Backus, Alan Perlis and Friedrich Bauer, met at ETH Zürich and produced the International Algebraic Language, known as ALGOL 58. In 1959 Backus presented a formal notation for its syntax at a UNESCO conference in Paris. In January 1960 thirteen delegates met in Paris and agreed ALGOL 60, and Peter Naur edited their report, refining Backus's notation into what became known as Backus-Naur form. In August 1960 Edsger Dijkstra and Jaap Zonneveld in Amsterdam finished the first ALGOL 60 compiler, for the Electrologica X1. From 1960 Communications of the ACM printed the algorithms it published in ALGOL.`,facts:[`The ALGOL 60 report had 13 authors from seven countries (1960).`,`Dijkstra and Zonneveld's 1960 compiler was among the first to handle recursion properly.`]},"tech:cobol":{intro:`COBOL, the Common Business-Oriented Language, is a language for business data processing: payrolls, invoices, stock and accounts. Programs are written in English-like sentences such as ADD TAX TO TOTAL, so managers could at least hope to read them. Its aim is that one program should run on computers from different makers with little change.`,how:`A COBOL program is split into four divisions: identification, environment for the particular machine, data describing every record field by field, and procedure for the actual steps. Records are laid out with level numbers and picture clauses, such as PICTURE 9(5)V99 for a money amount with two decimal places. Arithmetic is decimal, so pennies add up exactly as an accountant expects.`,history:`In May 1959 the US Department of Defense hosted a meeting at the Pentagon that set up CODASYL, the Conference on Data Systems Languages, to create one common business language. In 1959 its short-range committee borrowed heavily from Grace Hopper's FLOW-MATIC, already used on UNIVAC machines, and from IBM's COMTRAN. In 1960 the first specification, COBOL-60, was printed by the US Government Printing Office. In 1968 the American National Standards Institute published its first COBOL standard. By 1970 COBOL had become the most widely used programming language in the world.`,facts:[`The CODASYL committee drafted the first COBOL specification in about six months (1959).`,`By 1970 COBOL was the most widely used programming language in the world.`]},"tech:timesharing":{intro:`Time-sharing lets many people use one large computer at once, each from their own typewriter terminal. The machine gives each user a slice of a second in turn, so fast that everyone seems to have it to themselves. Programmers can type, run, see an error and fix it in minutes instead of waiting hours for a batch job to come back.`,how:`A clock interrupts the running program many times a second and hands control to a supervisor. The supervisor saves that user's state, swaps another user's program in from drum or disk if needed, and lets it run for a short slice. Memory protection stops one user's program touching another's, and a command language lets users log in, edit files and start programs.`,history:`In 1959 John McCarthy at MIT wrote a memo proposing that a large computer be shared by many users at terminals. In November 1961 Fernando Corbató's group at MIT demonstrated the Compatible Time-Sharing System, CTSS, on a modified IBM 709. In 1963 MIT's Project MAC, funded by the US military research agency ARPA, put CTSS into daily service on an IBM 7094. In 1965 CTSS gained a MAIL command, one of the first ways for users to leave messages for each other. By 1968 firms such as Tymshare were renting time on their computers to businesses by the hour over telephone lines.`,facts:[`CTSS on MIT's IBM 7094 served up to about 30 users at once (1963).`,`The MAIL command of 1965 on CTSS was one of the earliest forms of email.`]},"tech:virtmem":{intro:`Virtual memory makes a computer's small, fast core memory and its big, slow drum look like one large store to every program. Programs use addresses as if all of memory were theirs, while the hardware and supervisor quietly move blocks between drum and core as needed. Programmers no longer have to plan by hand which parts of a program sit in core at each moment.`,how:`Memory is divided into fixed-size pages, and a table records which page of the program sits in which frame of core. When a program touches a page that is out on the drum, the hardware stops it and calls the supervisor, which brings the page in, sends a little-used page out to make room, and restarts the instruction. On Atlas, a set of special registers checked every address against the pages in core.`,history:`In 1959 Tom Kilburn's team at Manchester University, working with Ferranti, began designing a 'one-level store' for the Atlas computer. In December 1962 Atlas was officially switched on, the first computer with paged virtual memory. In 1966 Laszlo Belady at IBM studied which page a system should throw out to cause the fewest delays. In 1968 Peter Denning's working-set model explained why systems slowed to a crawl, or thrashed, when too many programs competed for core. In 1972 IBM brought virtual memory to its whole System/370 range.`,facts:[`Atlas had 16,384 words of core memory backed by 98,304 words of drum (1962).`,`Each Atlas page held 512 words (1962).`,`Atlas was claimed to be the world's most powerful computer when it started in 1962.`]},"tech:basic":{intro:`BASIC, the Beginner's All-purpose Symbolic Instruction Code, is a programming language small and friendly enough for students who are not scientists. Each line starts with a number and holds one plain command, such as PRINT, LET, INPUT or GOTO. Typed at a time-sharing terminal, a program runs at once and the answer comes back in seconds.`,how:`Line numbers set the order of the program and let a user replace a line simply by typing it again with the same number. Commands such as RUN, LIST and SAVE are typed straight at the terminal. The Dartmouth system compiled each program in a flash whenever RUN was typed, so beginners got both instant feedback and good speed, and never saw a punched card.`,history:`In 1963 John Kemeny and Thomas Kurtz at Dartmouth College in New Hampshire started building a time-sharing system and a simple language so that every student, not only scientists, could use a computer. In May 1964 the first BASIC programs ran on the college's General Electric GE-225, with a smaller Datanet-30 machine handling the terminals. In 1964 Dartmouth chose to give BASIC away freely rather than sell it. In 1965 General Electric offered BASIC on its commercial time-sharing service, carrying it to companies and schools far beyond Dartmouth. By 1970 DEC and Hewlett-Packard sold minicomputers running their own versions of BASIC to schools.`,facts:[`The first BASIC of 1964 had fewer than 20 kinds of statement.`,`John Kemeny had worked as Albert Einstein's mathematical assistant at Princeton in 1948.`]},"tech:familyos":{intro:`A family operating system is a single control program meant to run on every model of a compatible range of computers, from small business machines to giant scientific ones. Customers can move up to a bigger model without rewriting their programs. It must handle batch jobs, many kinds of device and several programs at once, which makes it enormous and hard to finish.`,how:`OS/360 ran jobs described in Job Control Language, cards that named the programs, files and devices to use. Its supervisor juggled several jobs in memory at once and kept catalogues of data sets on disk and tape. Because it was too big for the smallest machines, IBM shipped simpler systems such as DOS/360 alongside it, so one system for all became a family of related ones.`,history:`In April 1964 IBM announced System/360, a range of compatible computers that would all run the same software, in what Fortune magazine called a $5 billion gamble. In 1965 Fred Brooks, who had managed the hardware and then OS/360, left IBM to found the computer science department at the University of North Carolina. In 1966 OS/360 began shipping, late and in a stripped-down first version. In 1967 IBM added versions that could run several jobs at once, in fixed or variable slices of memory. In 1975 Brooks drew lessons from the project in The Mythical Man-Month.`,facts:[`IBM spent about $5 billion developing System/360 (1964).`,`Brooks's 1975 book argued that adding people to a late software project makes it later.`]},"tech:database":{intro:`A database management system is one program that stores and looks after an organisation's records, so that every application asks it for data instead of keeping private files. It keeps related records linked, checks that they stay consistent and finds any record fast, even among millions. Programs no longer break each time a file's layout changes.`,how:`The first systems were navigational: records are linked by pointers into chains or trees, such as a customer record pointing to its orders. A program finds data by starting at an entry point and following the links one record at a time. A description of the data, kept apart from the programs, tells the system how records are laid out on disk, and one copy of each fact replaces dozens of duplicates.`,history:`In 1963 Charles Bachman at General Electric built the Integrated Data Store, IDS, which kept linked records on disk for many programs to share. In 1966 IBM, North American Aviation and Caterpillar began building a system to track the parts for the Apollo programme's Saturn V rocket, which became IBM's IMS, the Information Management System. In 1969 the CODASYL Data Base Task Group published a standard network database model based on Bachman's ideas. In 1973 Bachman received the Turing Award and gave a lecture titled 'The Programmer as Navigator'.`,facts:[`Bachman's IDS of 1963 ran on a General Electric GE 225 computer.`,`Bachman won the 1973 Turing Award for his work on databases.`]},"tech:objects":{intro:`Object-oriented programming bundles data together with the procedures that work on it into units called objects. A class is the template from which objects are made, and a new class can inherit everything from an older one and add to it, as a bus is a kind of vehicle. It began as a way to write simulations of queues, ships and factories.`,how:`In Simula 67 a programmer declares a class with its own variables and procedures, then creates as many objects of it as needed with the word new. A subclass is declared by putting the parent class's name in front, and it gets all the parent's attributes for free. Virtual procedures let a subclass replace a parent's behaviour, so code written for vehicles calls the right routine for each kind of vehicle.`,history:`In 1962 Kristen Nygaard and Ole-Johan Dahl at the Norwegian Computing Center in Oslo began work on Simula, a language for simulating systems such as queues at a bank or ships in a harbour. By 1965 the first Simula ran on the centre's UNIVAC 1107. In 1966 Tony Hoare's idea of record classes showed the pair how to make their processes into general-purpose objects. In May 1967, at a conference at Lysebu near Oslo, Dahl and Nygaard presented Simula 67 with classes, subclasses and virtual procedures. In 2001 both men received the Turing Award for ideas fundamental to object-oriented programming.`,facts:[`UNIVAC sold the Norwegian Computing Center a UNIVAC 1107 at a discount in return for Simula (1962).`,`Dahl and Nygaard won the 2001 Turing Award and both died within weeks of each other in 2002.`]},"tech:structprog":{intro:`Structured programming is a discipline for writing programs that people can read and check. Control flows through only three shapes: one step after another, a choice between alternatives, and a repeating loop, each with one way in and one way out. The tangled jumps of the GOTO statement, which make large programs impossible to follow, are avoided.`,how:`A programmer starts from the whole task and splits it into a few smaller steps, then refines each step the same way until every piece is simple enough to code directly. Because each block has one entry and one exit, it can be understood, tested and even proved correct on its own. Languages with good blocks, such as ALGOL, made the style natural; in FORTRAN it took self-discipline.`,history:`In 1966 Corrado Böhm and Giuseppe Jacopini proved that any program can be written using only sequence, choice and loops. In March 1968 Edsger Dijkstra's letter to Communications of the ACM, given the title 'Go To Statement Considered Harmful' by the editor Niklaus Wirth, set off years of argument. In 1971 Wirth's paper 'Program Development by Stepwise Refinement' showed how to design a program from the top down. In 1972 IBM reported that a team using these methods had built an information system for The New York Times with remarkably few errors. In 1974 Donald Knuth's paper 'Structured Programming with go to Statements' argued that a few careful jumps could still be clear.`,facts:[`Dijkstra's famous 1968 letter ran to just two pages.`,`Dijkstra received the Turing Award in 1972.`]},"tech:hypervisor":{intro:`A virtual machine is a complete imitation computer made of software, running on a real one. A thin control program, the hypervisor, gives each user what looks like a private machine with its own memory, disks and console, on which any operating system can be loaded. Users can crash or restart their machine without disturbing anyone else.`,how:`Guest operating systems run in a less privileged mode. Whenever a guest tries a privileged instruction, such as starting a disk transfer or changing its memory map, the hardware traps to the control program, which carries out a safe version and returns. At IBM's Cambridge centre each user ran a light single-user system, CMS, inside their own virtual machine, passing files through virtual card readers and printers.`,history:`In 1964, after MIT chose General Electric's machine for its next time-sharing project, IBM's Cambridge Scientific Center set out to show that IBM hardware could share itself well. In January 1967 Robert Creasy's team put CP-40 into use on a specially modified System/360 Model 40. In 1968 CP-67 ran on the standard Model 67, and IBM made it available to customers with its source code. In 1972 IBM turned it into the product VM/370 for its new System/370 range. In 1974 Gerald Popek and Robert Goldberg set out the formal conditions a processor must meet to be virtualised efficiently.`,facts:[`CP-40 could run up to 14 virtual machines at once on one System/360 Model 40 (1967).`,`Each CP-40 guest had 256 KB of virtual memory (1967).`]},"tech:softeng":{intro:`Software engineering is the idea that building large programs should be a disciplined engineering trade, like building bridges, with specifications, designs, reviews, testing and schedules. It grew from a sense of crisis: big projects were late, over budget and full of errors. Its supporters argued that programming needed methods, measurement and management, not just clever individuals.`,how:`Work is split into phases: gather requirements, write a specification, design the modules, code them, test them and then maintain the result. Each phase ends with documents that others review before the next begins. Managers estimate effort in lines of code and staff-months, and keep records of the errors found, so the next project can be planned better.`,history:`In October 1968 the NATO Science Committee gathered more than 50 experts from 11 countries at Garmisch in Germany, under a deliberately provocative title: 'Software Engineering'. In 1969 Peter Naur and Brian Randell published the conference report, and a second conference followed in Rome. In 1970 Winston Royce of TRW described a sequence of development phases and warned that doing them strictly in one pass invited failure. In 1981 Barry Boehm's book Software Engineering Economics introduced the COCOMO model for estimating cost from program size. In 1984 the US government funded a Software Engineering Institute at Carnegie Mellon University.`,facts:[`More than 50 people from 11 countries attended the Garmisch conference (1968).`,`Boehm's COCOMO model of 1981 estimated staff-months from thousands of lines of code.`]},"tech:filesystems":{intro:`A hierarchical file system arranges files in directories that can hold other directories, forming a tree that every user navigates by path names. Each file and directory carries rules about who may read, change or run it. Together these let hundreds of users share one computer and its data safely, keeping private what should stay private.`,how:`Every file has a path, such as >udd>project>smith>notes, read from the root directory down. Each entry carries an access control list naming which users or groups may read, write or execute it. In Multics, files were not copied into memory but mapped straight into a program's address space as segments, and hardware rings of protection kept user programs away from the supervisor.`,history:`In 1964 MIT's Project MAC, General Electric and Bell Labs began designing Multics, the Multiplexed Information and Computing Service, as a computer utility as dependable as electricity. In 1965 the team presented six papers at the Fall Joint Computer Conference, including Robert Daley and Peter Neumann's design for a tree-structured file system. In April 1969 Bell Labs pulled out of the project, frustrated by its delays. In October 1969 Multics went into general service at MIT on a GE 645. In 1970 Honeywell bought General Electric's computer business and took over Multics.`,facts:[`Multics was written mostly in PL/I, a high-level language, unusual for an operating system in 1969.`,`In 1985 Multics became the first operating system rated B2 for security by the US government.`,`The last Multics system, at Canada's Department of National Defence, shut down in 2000.`]},"tech:relational":{intro:`The relational model stores data as simple tables of rows and columns, called relations, and links them by matching values rather than by pointers. A user states what data is wanted, for example all orders from customers in Leeds, and the system works out how to find it. Programs no longer depend on how records happen to be laid out on disk.`,how:`Each table has a key column that identifies its rows, such as a customer number. A small set of operations combines tables: select the rows that meet a condition, project chosen columns, and join two tables wherever their keys match. Because the model rests on set theory and logic, its results can be reasoned about precisely, and a system is free to reorganise storage or add indexes without breaking any program.`,history:`In 1969 Edgar Codd, an Oxford-trained mathematician at IBM's San Jose laboratory, set out the idea in an internal IBM research report. In June 1970 Communications of the ACM published his paper 'A Relational Model of Data for Large Shared Data Banks'. In the early 1970s IBM, earning well from its navigational IMS database, was slow to act on the idea. In 1973 IBM started System R in San Jose, and Michael Stonebraker and Eugene Wong began Ingres at the University of California, Berkeley, both to prove the model could perform. In 1974 Codd debated Charles Bachman, champion of navigational databases, at an ACM workshop in what became known as the Great Debate.`,facts:[`Codd's 1970 paper filled 11 pages of Communications of the ACM.`,`Codd had joined IBM in 1949 as a programming mathematician.`]},"tech:pascal":{intro:`Pascal is a compact programming language designed for teaching programming as a clear, systematic discipline. It has strong data types, records, sets and the structured statements if, while, repeat and case, and it catches many mistakes when the program is compiled. It is small enough that one person can understand the whole language and write its compiler.`,how:`Every variable must be declared with its type before use, and the compiler refuses to mix, say, a character and a number. Programmers can define their own types, such as a record holding a name and an age, or a range from 1 to 12. The compiler was itself written in Pascal and worked in a single pass through the program text, so compiling was quick.`,history:`In 1966 Niklaus Wirth's proposal for the next version of ALGOL was passed over by the international committee, and he built it at Stanford as ALGOL W. In 1970 his new language Pascal, named after the French mathematician Blaise Pascal, ran on a CDC 6000 computer at ETH Zürich. In 1974 Kathleen Jensen and Wirth published the Pascal User Manual and Report, which became the language's reference. In 1981 Brian Kernighan's essay 'Why Pascal Is Not My Favorite Programming Language' listed its limits for practical work. In 1984 the US Advanced Placement exam in computer science was first set in Pascal, and Wirth received the Turing Award.`,facts:[`The first Pascal compiler ran on a CDC 6000 at ETH Zürich in 1970.`,`From 1984 the US Advanced Placement computer science exam used Pascal.`]},"tech:unix":{intro:`Unix is a small, simple time-sharing operating system written by two Bell Labs researchers for a minicomputer. Files are plain sequences of bytes in a tree of directories, devices look like files too, and a command interpreter, the shell, runs small programs that each do one job well. It offers much of what far larger systems promised, on far cheaper hardware.`,how:`The kernel offers a handful of system calls: open, read, write and close a file, fork a new process, and exec a program. Everything else, from the editor to the assembler, is an ordinary program started by the shell. Each file has an owner and simple read, write and execute permissions for the owner and for others. The first versions were written in assembly language for DEC's PDP-7 and PDP-11.`,history:`In 1969, after Bell Labs left the Multics project, Ken Thompson wrote a simple operating system on a little-used DEC PDP-7, soon joined by Dennis Ritchie. In 1970 Brian Kernighan suggested the name Unix, a pun on Multics, and the team won a PDP-11 by promising a text-processing system for the patent department. In 1973 Doug McIlroy's pipes arrived in the Third Edition, letting the output of one program flow straight into another. In July 1974 Thompson and Ritchie's paper in Communications of the ACM made the system famous. In 1975 AT&T, barred by a 1956 court settlement from selling computers, was licensing Unix with source code to universities for a small fee.`,facts:[`Unix's first PDP-11 had 24 KB of memory: 16 KB for the system, 8 KB for users (1971).`,`Ritchie and Thompson won the Turing Award in 1983 for Unix.`]},"tech:clang":{intro:`C is a compact programming language for writing systems software, such as operating systems, compilers and editors. It gives direct access to memory through pointers and maps neatly onto the instructions of a minicomputer, yet it has structured statements, functions and data types. A program written in C can be moved to another kind of computer far more easily than one in assembly language.`,how:`C has a small core of types: characters, integers and floating-point numbers, with arrays, pointers and structures built from them. Pointers hold memory addresses, and arithmetic on them lets a program walk quickly through arrays and buffers. The language leaves out input and output, string handling and memory checks; those come from a library or from the programmer's care, which keeps the compiler small and the code fast.`,history:`In 1969 Ken Thompson created B, a stripped-down language based on Martin Richards's BCPL from Cambridge, for the PDP-7. In 1971 Dennis Ritchie began adding data types to B for the PDP-11, calling the result NB and then, in 1972, C. In 1973 Ritchie and Thompson rewrote the Unix kernel in C, one of the first operating systems written in a high-level language. In 1977 Steve Johnson's Portable C Compiler helped Ritchie and Johnson move Unix to a quite different computer, the Interdata 8/32. In 1978 Kernighan and Ritchie's book The C Programming Language opened with a program that printed 'hello, world'. In 1989 the American National Standards Institute approved a standard for C.`,facts:[`The 1973 Unix kernel in C was only about a third larger than the assembly version.`,`ANSI approved its C standard in 1989.`]},"tech:versioncontrol":{intro:`Version control keeps a history of every change to a program's source files: what changed, when, by whom and why. Any earlier version can be rebuilt on demand, so a bad change can be undone and a released version can be fixed while new work goes on. It replaces piles of dated copies and handwritten change logs.`,how:`SCCS stores each file once, with every change woven in as numbered deltas that mark which version added or removed each line. To get a version, the tool reads the file and keeps only the lines that belonged to it. A programmer checks a file out for editing, which locks it so nobody else changes it at the same time, then checks it back in with a comment explaining the change.`,history:`In 1972 Marc Rochkind at Bell Labs wrote the Source Code Control System, SCCS, to keep track of changes to programs. In 1975 Rochkind described it in IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering. In 1977 SCCS shipped with AT&T's Programmer's Workbench version of Unix, spreading it across Bell System software projects. In 1982 Walter Tichy at Purdue University released RCS, the Revision Control System, which stored the newest version in full and older ones as reverse differences, so fetching the latest version was fast. In 1986 RCS became the base on which the first version of CVS was built.`,facts:[`SCCS shipped with the Programmer's Workbench Unix in 1977.`,`Rochkind's 1975 SCCS paper appeared in the first volume of IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering.`]},"tech:dct":{intro:`The discrete cosine transform rewrites a block of samples, such as an 8×8 square of pixels, as a mix of smooth cosine waves of rising frequency. In most pictures nearly all the energy lands in a few low-frequency waves, so the rest can be stored coarsely or dropped. It is a key to squeezing photographs and video far more than lossless methods can.`,how:`The transform turns 64 pixel values into 64 coefficients: one for the block's average brightness and others for gradually finer patterns. Quantising divides each coefficient by a step size and rounds it, with big steps for the fine detail the eye barely notices. Many coefficients become zero and cost almost nothing to store. The decoder multiplies back and runs the inverse transform; the loss is controlled and usually hard to see.`,history:`In 1972 Nasir Ahmed at Kansas State University proposed the discrete cosine transform for compressing images, but the US National Science Foundation turned down his grant request. In January 1974 Ahmed, T Natarajan and K R Rao published it in IEEE Transactions on Computers, showing it came close to the ideal but impractical Karhunen-Loeve transform. In 1977 Wen-Hsiung Chen and colleagues published a fast way of computing it. In 1988 the CCITT's H.261 video standard for video calls adopted it. In 1992 the JPEG standard used the 8×8 DCT for photographs.`,facts:[`Ahmed's 1972 grant proposal for the DCT was rejected as too simple.`,`The JPEG photo standard of 1992 and MPEG video of 1993 were both built on the DCT.`]},"tech:sql":{intro:`SQL is a language for asking questions of a relational database in something close to plain English. A user writes what result is wanted, which tables to look in and which rows to keep, and the database decides how to fetch it. The same language adds, changes and deletes rows and defines new tables, so people who are not programmers can work with data directly.`,how:`A query has a few clauses: SELECT the columns, FROM the tables, WHERE the conditions hold. Joining tables is done simply by naming two in FROM and matching their keys in WHERE. Results can be grouped and totalled or sorted. The database itself decides the order in which to read the tables and which indexes to use, so the same query keeps working as the data grows.`,history:`In 1973 Donald Chamberlin and Raymond Boyce at IBM's San Jose lab, inspired by Codd's relational model, set out to design a query language that people without mathematical training could use. In 1974 they published SEQUEL, the Structured English Query Language, as part of IBM's System R project. In 1974 Boyce died suddenly of a brain aneurysm, aged just 26. In the late 1970s IBM shortened the name to SQL because SEQUEL was already a trademark of the British aircraft maker Hawker Siddeley. In 1979 Patricia Selinger's team described System R's optimiser, which estimated the cost of each possible plan and picked the cheapest. In 1987 SQL became an international ISO standard.`,facts:[`Raymond Boyce, SQL's co-designer, died in 1974 aged 26.`,`SQL became an ANSI standard in 1986 and an ISO standard in 1987.`]},"tech:micro_os":{intro:`A microcomputer operating system is a small program, a few kilobytes in size, that lets a computer built around a single-chip processor keep files on floppy disks and run programs typed at a terminal. It gives programmers a simple set of services, such as read a sector, open a file or print a character, so their software need not know how each machine's hardware works.`,how:`CP/M has three parts: a command processor for typed commands such as DIR and TYPE, a disk operating system that manages files and directories, and a small layer holding the only code specific to each machine's console and disk drives. Moving it to a new computer means rewriting only that layer. Programs load at a fixed address and ask for services by calling one entry point with a function number.`,history:`In 1974 Gary Kildall, who taught at the Naval Postgraduate School in Monterey, California, wrote CP/M, the Control Program for Microcomputers, for Intel's 8080, using his own PL/M language. In 1976 Kildall and his wife Dorothy McEwen founded Intergalactic Digital Research, soon just Digital Research, to sell it. In 1977 IMSAI paid $25,000 to put CP/M on its computers, and other makers followed. In 1980 IBM approached Digital Research about an operating system for its coming personal computer, but the two did not reach a deal, and IBM turned to Microsoft. In 1981 Microsoft's PC DOS, based on Tim Paterson's CP/M-like 86-DOS, shipped with the IBM PC.`,facts:[`IMSAI paid Digital Research a flat $25,000 to license CP/M in 1977.`,`PL/M, the language CP/M was written in, was itself one Kildall had created for Intel in 1973.`]},"tech:rombasic":{intro:`A BASIC interpreter is a whole programming language squeezed into a few kilobytes, so a small personal computer can be programmed by its owner straight away. The interpreter reads each line as the program runs and carries it out directly, with no separate compile step. Typing a line without a number makes it run at once, like a calculator.`,how:`To save memory, each keyword such as PRINT or GOTO is stored as a single byte, a token, when the line is entered. When the program runs, the interpreter reads the tokens one by one, looks up variables in a table and calls its built-in routines for arithmetic and printing. The floating-point maths alone takes a large share of the space. On many machines the interpreter is built into read-only memory, ready the moment the power comes on.`,history:`In January 1975 Popular Electronics put the MITS Altair 8800 kit on its cover, and Bill Gates and Paul Allen offered its maker a BASIC they had not yet written. In early 1975 they wrote it at Harvard on a PDP-10 programmed to imitate the 8080 chip, with Monte Davidoff writing the floating-point routines. In March 1975 Allen loaded it from paper tape into a real Altair in Albuquerque, and it worked. In February 1976 Gates's 'Open Letter to Hobbyists' complained that most users had copied it without paying. In 1977 the Commodore PET came with Microsoft BASIC in read-only memory, and Microsoft versions for the Apple II and TRS-80 followed in 1978.`,facts:[`The first Altair BASIC fitted in 4 KB of memory (1975).`,`Gates's 1976 letter said the computer time used to write Altair BASIC was worth over $40,000.`]},"tech:smalltalk":{intro:`Smalltalk is both a programming language and a complete, living computer environment in which everything, from numbers to windows, is an object that responds to messages. The system shows overlapping windows, pop-up menus and a mouse-driven browser of its own code. Any part of it, even the compiler, can be read and changed while it runs.`,how:`Instead of calling a function, a Smalltalk program sends a message to an object, such as 3 + 4 or window close, and the object decides how to respond. Classes are objects too, arranged in a hierarchy that a programmer explores in a browser. The whole state of the system, every object in memory, can be saved as an image and resumed exactly as it was, so programming feels like reshaping a world rather than editing files.`,history:`In 1972 Alan Kay's Learning Research Group at Xerox PARC built the first Smalltalk, with Dan Ingalls writing its interpreter, as part of Kay's vision of the Dynabook, a book-sized computer for children. In 1973 Xerox's Alto, with its bitmapped screen and mouse, gave Smalltalk a home, and children from local schools came to PARC to program in it. In 1976 Ingalls redesigned the language as Smalltalk-76, adding inheritance and a much faster compiler. In December 1979 Steve Jobs and a team from Apple visited PARC and saw Smalltalk's windows and menus. In August 1981 Byte magazine devoted a whole issue to Smalltalk-80, bringing its ideas to a wide audience.`,facts:[`The Alto's screen showed 606 by 808 pixels, taller than wide like a page (1973).`,`Alan Kay received the Turing Award in 2003.`]},"tech:transactions":{intro:`A transaction is a group of database changes that succeeds or fails as one unit, such as taking money from one account and adding it to another. Either every change happens or none does, and other users never see the job half done. If the computer crashes, the database can recover to a correct state, losing nothing it had promised was finished.`,how:`Before changing any data, the system writes a note of the change to a log. A transaction counts as committed only when its log records are safely on disk, so after a crash the system replays committed work from the log and undoes unfinished work. Locks stop two transactions from changing the same record at once, and the system detects deadlocks, where two jobs wait for each other forever, and cancels one.`,history:`In 1976 Kapali Eswaran, Jim Gray, Raymond Lorie and Irving Traiger of IBM's System R team showed that two-phase locking keeps many users' transactions from interfering with each other. In 1976 Tandem Computers shipped its first NonStop system, built so that banks' transaction processing would survive the failure of any single part. In 1978 Gray's 'Notes on Data Base Operating Systems' described write-ahead logging and the two-phase commit protocol for transactions spanning several computers. In 1981 Gray's paper 'The Transaction Concept' set out the guarantees in plain terms. In 1983 Theo Härder and Andreas Reuter summed them up in the acronym ACID: atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability.`,facts:[`The acronym ACID was coined in 1983 by Theo Härder and Andreas Reuter.`,`Jim Gray received the Turing Award in 1998 for his work on transactions.`]},"tech:make":{intro:`Build automation means a tool, not a person, works out which parts of a program must be recompiled after a change, and does it. The programmer writes a file listing each target, the files it depends on and the command that makes it. Forgotten steps and hours of needless recompiling both disappear.`,how:`A makefile holds rules like: prog depends on main.o and util.o, and is built with cc. Make compares the modification times of each target and its dependencies; if any dependency is newer, it runs the command, working from the bottom of the tree upwards. Built-in rules already know how to turn a .c file into a .o file, so most makefiles stay short.`,history:`In April 1976 Stuart Feldman at Bell Labs wrote make after a colleague lost a morning debugging a program that had not actually been recompiled. In 1979 Feldman described it in the journal Software: Practice and Experience, and it shipped with Seventh Edition Unix. In 1988 Richard Stallman and Roland McGrath released GNU make, which added many features. In 2003 Feldman received the ACM Software System Award for make.`,facts:[`Make shipped to the wider world with Seventh Edition Unix in 1979.`,`Feldman won the 2003 ACM Software System Award for make.`]},"tech:screeneditor":{intro:`A screen editor shows a whole page of a file on a video terminal and lets the user move a cursor to any spot and type changes in place, seeing the result at once. It replaces line editors, where a programmer printed a line, typed a command to change it and printed it again. It needs terminals that can move the cursor anywhere on the screen.`,how:`The editor keeps a copy of the file in memory and redraws only the parts of the screen that change, sending the control codes each terminal model understands. In vi, keys switch between a command mode, where h, j, k and l move the cursor and dd deletes a line, and an insert mode for typing text. Emacs instead uses control-key combinations and lets users extend the editor with their own macros.`,history:`In 1976 Bill Joy, a graduate student at Berkeley, added a full-screen visual mode to the ex line editor, working on Lear Siegler ADM-3A terminals over slow lines. In 1976 Richard Stallman and Guy Steele at MIT's AI Lab gathered editing macros for the TECO editor into a package called Emacs, for Editor MACroS. By 1979 vi was distributed with Berkeley Unix and spread to Unix sites everywhere. In 1985 Stallman released GNU Emacs, programmable in its own dialect of LISP. In 1991 Bram Moolenaar released Vim, an improved copy of vi.`,facts:[`Vi's h, j, k and l keys matched the arrows printed on the ADM-3A terminal's keyboard (1976).`,`GNU Emacs, released in 1985, was among the first programs of the GNU project.`]},"tech:arithcoding":{intro:`Arithmetic coding packs a whole message into a single long binary fraction between 0 and 1. Each symbol narrows the range in proportion to how likely it is, so a very common symbol costs only a fraction of a bit. It squeezes data almost exactly to the limit information theory allows, which codes made of whole bits, like Huffman's, cannot always reach.`,how:`Start with the interval from 0 to 1. Divide it into slices, one per possible symbol, sized by probability, and keep the slice of the symbol that actually comes; then repeat for the next symbol inside that slice. Any number inside the final tiny interval identifies the whole message. Practical coders use whole-number arithmetic and send out leading bits as soon as they are settled, so they never need endless precision.`,history:`In the early 1960s Peter Elias of MIT had sketched the idea, but it seemed to need impossibly precise arithmetic. In 1976 Jorma Rissanen at IBM and Richard Pasco at Stanford independently found ways to do it with limited-precision numbers. In 1979 Rissanen and Glen Langdon of IBM generalised the method, and IBM patented key parts of it. In 1987 Ian Witten, Radford Neal and John Cleary published a clear, working program for it in Communications of the ACM, bringing it to ordinary programmers. In 1992 JPEG included an arithmetic-coding option, but patents held by IBM and others meant almost nobody used it.`,facts:[`Witten, Neal and Cleary's 1987 paper printed a complete arithmetic coder in C.`,`JPEG's 1992 arithmetic mode made files about 5 to 10 per cent smaller than its Huffman mode.`]},"tech:lz":{intro:`Dictionary compression shrinks data by replacing repeated strings with short references to an earlier copy. It needs no table of letter frequencies prepared in advance: the compressor learns the data's repeats as it goes, so the same method works on English, program code or pictures. Decoding is simple and very fast.`,how:`In the LZ77 scheme the compressor keeps a window of recent text and, at each point, searches it for the longest match; it outputs a pair, distance back and length, or a literal character when nothing matches. In LZ78 both sides instead build identical dictionaries of phrases seen so far, and the compressor sends the number of a known phrase plus the next character, which becomes a new entry.`,history:`In 1977 Abraham Lempel and Jacob Ziv published 'A Universal Algorithm for Sequential Data Compression' in IEEE Transactions on Information Theory. Their 1977 proof showed that on long enough data the method approaches the best compression possible for that source, without knowing its statistics in advance. In 1978 they followed with LZ78, which built an explicit dictionary. In 1982 James Storer and Thomas Szymanski improved LZ77 so it sent single characters whenever a match was too short to be worth it. In 2021 Ziv received the IEEE Medal of Honor for the work, and both inventors died in 2023.`,facts:[`Jacob Ziv received the IEEE Medal of Honor in 2021 for the Lempel-Ziv algorithms.`,`LZSS, the 1982 variant, marks each item with one flag bit: a literal or a copy.`]},"tech:bytecode":{intro:`Portable bytecode is code compiled for an imaginary, simple computer rather than for a real chip. A small interpreter, written once for each real machine, pretends to be that imaginary computer and runs the code. The compiler, the editor and every program written for the system then move to a new computer just by writing that one interpreter.`,how:`Pascal's p-code is the instruction set of a stack machine: instructions push values, add the top two, store into variables or call procedures, each packed into a byte or two. The code is compact, which suits small memories, though interpreting it runs several times slower than native code. The UCSD p-System bundled the interpreter with an editor, a file manager and the compiler into a whole operating system.`,history:`In 1974 Niklaus Wirth's group at ETH Zürich distributed Pascal-P, a compiler that produced p-code for an imaginary stack machine, so universities could port Pascal by writing only an interpreter. In 1977 Kenneth Bowles at the University of California, San Diego built UCSD Pascal on the same idea, aiming to give students a complete system on cheap microcomputers. In 1979 Apple sold it as Apple Pascal for the Apple II, and SofTech Microsystems took over its commercial sales. In 1981 IBM offered the p-System as one of three operating systems for its new PC, alongside PC DOS and CP/M-86.`,facts:[`Apple Pascal, based on the UCSD p-System, went on sale for the Apple II in 1979.`,`The IBM PC of 1981 offered the p-System as one of its three operating systems.`]},"tech:bsd":{intro:`Berkeley Unix is a version of Bell Labs' Unix extended by students and staff at the University of California, Berkeley, and shipped on tape to other licence holders. It adds new tools, such as a screen editor and a Pascal system, and supports newer hardware. A university group shares its improvements openly, so the system grows quickly through outside help.`,how:`Each Berkeley Software Distribution came on magnetic tape as source code, for sites that already held an AT&T Unix licence. The first tapes carried add-on programs for the PDP-11, such as the ex editor and a Pascal compiler. Because users received the source, they could fix bugs and send changes back to Berkeley, and the best were folded into the next tape.`,history:`In 1974 Professor Bob Fabry obtained a Unix licence for Berkeley, and in 1975 Ken Thompson spent a year there as a visiting professor. In March 1978 graduate student Bill Joy sent out the first Berkeley Software Distribution, about 30 copies. In 1979 3BSD brought paged virtual memory to DEC's new VAX computers. In 1980 the US military research agency DARPA chose Berkeley Unix for its research network, funding the work that put TCP/IP networking into 4.2BSD in 1983. In 1982 Joy left to co-found Sun Microsystems. In 1994 4.4BSD-Lite, free of AT&T code, came out after a lawsuit by AT&T's Unix company was settled.`,facts:[`About 30 copies of the first Berkeley tape were sent out in 1978.`,`Bill Joy co-founded Sun Microsystems in 1982, taking Berkeley Unix with him.`]},"tech:rdbms":{intro:`A commercial SQL database is a relational database system sold as a product: it stores tables, answers SQL queries and protects data with transactions and recovery. Companies can buy what IBM's research prototype proved possible, instead of building navigational databases by hand. It runs on minicomputers as well as mainframes, so smaller firms can afford one.`,how:`The database runs as a server process that owns the data files. Programs and users send it SQL statements; it parses each one, picks a plan, uses indexes to find rows and returns the results. A catalogue of tables, columns and indexes is itself stored in tables that SQL can query. Logs and locks keep many users' changes safe, and the same SQL works whatever the hardware underneath.`,history:`In 1977 Larry Ellison, Bob Miner and Ed Oates founded Software Development Laboratories in California, after reading IBM's published papers on relational databases. In 1979 the company, renamed Relational Software Inc, released Oracle for DEC's PDP-11, beating IBM to market with an SQL product. In 1980 Relational Technology Inc began selling Ingres, the Berkeley research system turned into a product. In 1982 the company renamed itself Oracle after its database. In 1983 IBM's DB2 brought SQL to its biggest mainframes. In 1986 Oracle went public on the stock market, a day before Microsoft.`,facts:[`Larry Ellison led Oracle as chief executive from its founding in 1977 until 2014.`,`Oracle went public in 1986, one day before Microsoft.`]},"tech:ide":{intro:`An integrated development environment puts the editor, the compiler and the run command in one program, so a programmer never leaves it. Write code, press one key, and in seconds the program compiles and runs; if there is an error, the editor opens with the cursor on the faulty line. The slow round of edit, save, compile, link and reload shrinks to a moment.`,how:`Turbo Pascal kept the editor, the compiler and the program's source all in memory at once. Its compiler was a fast single-pass design that wrote machine code straight into memory, skipping a separate linking step. The editor used the same keystrokes as WordStar, the most popular word processor, so many users already knew them. The whole package fitted on one floppy disk with room to spare.`,history:`In 1983 Borland, a small firm founded by Philippe Kahn in Scotts Valley, California, launched Turbo Pascal, built on a compiler written by Anders Hejlsberg in Denmark. In 1983 it sold by mail order for a fraction of the price of rival compilers and quickly became a best-seller. In 1987 Borland followed with Turbo C, and Microsoft answered with QuickC. In 1997 Microsoft bundled its programming tools into Visual Studio. In 2001 IBM released Eclipse, a free and open-source development environment.`,facts:[`Turbo Pascal 1.0, editor and compiler together, took up under 40 KB (1983).`,`Borland's Turbo C appeared in 1987.`]},"tech:netos":{intro:`A network operating system turns one personal computer into a file server that shares its hard disk and printers with other PCs over a local cable. Users see the server's disk as another drive letter, and their files and print jobs travel over the network. Small offices get shared data without buying a minicomputer.`,how:`The server runs a special operating system tuned for serving files, with its own fast file system, disk caching and user accounts. Each PC loads a small redirector program that catches requests for network drives and sends them to the server instead of the local disk. Access rights decide who may open which directory, and the server queues everyone's print jobs onto shared printers.`,history:`In 1983 Novell of Provo, Utah, released NetWare, which ran a dedicated PC as a file server for other PCs on a local network. In 1983 Ray Noorda became Novell's chief executive and turned it from a struggling hardware maker into a networking software company. In 1987 Microsoft and 3Com announced LAN Manager to compete. In 1989 NetWare 386 used Intel's 32-bit chip to serve far more users from one machine. In 1993 NetWare 4 added a directory of every user and device across a whole company, the same year Microsoft launched Windows NT as a rival.`,facts:[`NetWare 386 of 1989 could serve up to 250 users from one server.`,`Ray Noorda took charge of Novell in 1983.`]},"tech:freesoftware":{intro:`Free software is software that every user may run, study, share and change, with its source code available. The idea is about freedom, not price: in Richard Stallman's phrase, think of free speech, not free beer. Its licences use copyright itself to keep the code free, a trick called copyleft.`,how:`A copyleft licence grants everyone the right to copy, modify and pass on the program, on one condition: any version passed on must carry the same licence and come with its source code. Nobody can take the code private. The GNU project set out to write a complete Unix-compatible system piece by piece: editor, compiler, debugger, shell and libraries, and finally a kernel.`,history:`In 1980 a Xerox laser printer at MIT whose driver source was withheld helped convince Richard Stallman that closed code took away users' freedom. In September 1983 Stallman, a programmer at MIT's AI Lab, announced the GNU project to build a complete free operating system compatible with Unix. In January 1984 he left his MIT job so the university could not claim GNU's code. In 1985 he published the GNU Manifesto, released GNU Emacs and founded the Free Software Foundation. In 1989 the first GNU General Public License replaced the separate licences of programs such as Emacs and GCC. In 1991 version 2 of the GPL appeared.`,facts:[`Stallman funded early GNU work in 1985 partly by selling Emacs tapes for $150 each.`,`Linux adopted GPL version 2 in 1992.`]},"tech:objc":{intro:`Objective-C adds Smalltalk-style objects and messages to the C language. Programmers keep C's speed and its large body of existing code, while building programs from reusable classes. Because each message is looked up while the program runs, an object can be replaced by any other that understands the same messages, which makes software components easy to swap and combine.`,how:`Ordinary C code and object code mix in one file. A message is written in square brackets, such as [shape drawAt: point], and the runtime finds the matching method in the receiver's class, or its parents, when the line runs. The compiler turns each message into a call to a small, fast lookup routine. Brad Cox sold libraries of classes as software ICs, like chips for programs.`,history:`In 1981 Brad Cox, a programmer at ITT, began adding Smalltalk's messages to C after reading about Smalltalk. In 1983 Cox and Tom Love founded Productivity Products International, renamed Stepstone, to sell the language. In 1986 Cox published Object-Oriented Programming: An Evolutionary Approach, explaining his software IC idea. In 1988 Steve Jobs's NeXT licensed Objective-C and built its NeXTSTEP system and developer tools in it. In 1995 NeXT bought the rights to the language from Stepstone. In 1996 Apple agreed to buy NeXT, bringing Objective-C to the Mac.`,facts:[`Apple agreed to pay $429 million for NeXT and its Objective-C system in 1996.`,`NeXT licensed Objective-C in 1988 for its whole operating system.`]},"tech:guios":{intro:`A graphical operating system shows files, programs and documents as pictures on screen: icons, windows, menus and a pointer moved by a mouse. Users point and click instead of memorising typed commands, and every program shares the same look and controls. What you see on screen matches what will print, fonts and all.`,how:`The screen is a bitmap, every dot controlled by software, so text in many fonts and pictures can mix freely. A window manager keeps track of overlapping windows and which parts need redrawing. Programs are built around an event loop: they wait for clicks and keystrokes and respond to each. A shared toolbox of menus, buttons, dialog boxes and scroll bars gives every program the same controls.`,history:`In 1973 Xerox PARC's Alto showed windows and a mouse on a bitmapped screen, though Xerox never sold it as a product. In 1981 the Xerox Star introduced the desktop, with icons for documents and folders, but it was priced for big companies. In 1983 Apple's Lisa brought the idea to a personal computer and sold poorly at its high price. In January 1984 the Macintosh made it affordable, launched with a famous Super Bowl advertisement. In 1985 Microsoft shipped Windows 1.0 as an add-on to MS-DOS. In 1990 Windows 3.0 finally made graphical PCs the everyday choice for offices.`,facts:[`A Xerox Star workstation cost about $16,000 in 1981.`,`Apple's Lisa cost $9,995 in 1983.`,`The first Macintosh had 128 KB of memory and cost $2,495 (1984).`]},"tech:lzw":{intro:`LZW, named after Lempel, Ziv and Welch, is a simple, fast form of dictionary compression. It starts with a dictionary holding every single character, and as it reads it adds each new phrase it meets. It sends only dictionary numbers, so it needs no frequency tables and is quick enough to run inside a disk controller chip.`,how:`The compressor reads characters while the string so far is in the dictionary. When adding the next character makes a new string, it outputs the code of the known part, adds the new string as the next entry and starts again from that character. The decoder rebuilds exactly the same dictionary from the codes alone. Codes typically start at 9 bits and grow longer as the dictionary fills.`,history:`In June 1983 Terry Welch of Sperry Research filed a patent on his refinement of LZ78. In June 1984 he described it in IEEE Computer as 'A Technique for High-Performance Data Compression'. In 1985 the Unix compress program used LZW and replaced weaker tools. In 1986 Sperry merged with Burroughs to form Unisys, which inherited the patent. In 1987 CompuServe's GIF image format chose LZW to shrink pictures for modem users. In December 1994 Unisys and CompuServe announced that software writing GIF files would owe royalties, angering programmers worldwide.`,facts:[`GIF, created by CompuServe in 1987, used LZW codes of up to 12 bits.`,`The Unix compress program of 1985 typically halved the size of text files.`]},"tech:case":{intro:`CASE, computer-aided software engineering, means using software to help design software. Analysts draw data-flow diagrams, entity-relationship charts and structure charts on a workstation, and the tool stores them in a shared repository, checks that they agree with each other, and sometimes generates database definitions and program code. It promised to turn programming into true engineering.`,how:`Upper CASE tools support analysis and design: drawing diagrams, defining every data item once in a dictionary, and checking for gaps, such as a process with no output. Lower CASE tools generate code, often COBOL, from the design and help to test it. Integrated tools tried to link both through a central repository, so a change in a diagram would flow through to the generated programs.`,history:`In 1978 Tom DeMarco's book Structured Analysis and System Specification spread data-flow diagrams through business computing. In 1984 Index Technology's Excelerator let analysts draw such diagrams on an IBM PC. In the late 1980s KnowledgeWare, led by the former American football quarterback Fran Tarkenton, sold its Information Engineering Workbench to large firms. In 1989 IBM announced AD/Cycle, a plan to tie CASE tools together around a central repository on its mainframes. In the early 1990s the boom faded, as the tools proved costly and their generated code hard to maintain. In 1994 the struggling KnowledgeWare was sold to Sterling Software.`,facts:[`IBM announced AD/Cycle in 1989 to unite CASE tools on its mainframes.`,`KnowledgeWare was sold to Sterling Software in 1994.`]},"tech:archiver":{intro:`A file archiver packs many files, with their names, dates and folders, into one compressed file. Software and documents can then travel on a few floppy disks or over a slow modem to a bulletin board, and be unpacked intact at the other end. It also saves space for files that are kept but rarely used.`,how:`The archiver reads each file, compresses it with the best of several methods, such as LZW, and writes it into the archive after a header holding its name, size, date and a check value. The check value, a CRC, lets the unpacker spot a file damaged in a download. Files are stored one after another, so the unpacker can list the contents or pull out a single file by skipping over the rest.`,history:`In 1985 Thom Henderson's System Enhancement Associates in New Jersey released ARC for MS-DOS, and it became the standard on bulletin board systems. In 1986 Phil Katz in Milwaukee released PKARC, a compatible program written in fast assembly language. In 1988 SEA sued Katz for copying, and he settled, agreeing to stop making ARC-compatible software. In 1989 Katz released PKZIP and published the ZIP format openly, and bulletin boards switched to it within months. In 1991 WinZip brought ZIP files to Windows with a point-and-click interface. In 1993 Eugene Roshal in Russia released RAR, which packed tighter still.`,facts:[`A standard PC floppy disk held just 360 KB in 1985.`,`WinZip, written by Nico Mak, brought ZIP files to Windows in 1991.`]},"tech:cpp":{intro:`C++ extends the C language with classes, so programs can be built from objects as in Simula while keeping C's speed and access to the machine. Existing C code still compiles, and features cost nothing unless they are used. It suits large systems, such as telephone switches, that need both structure and performance.`,how:`A class bundles data with member functions and controls who may see its insides by marking members public or private. Constructors set up an object and destructors clean it up when it goes out of scope, which ties resources like memory and files to object lifetimes. Virtual functions let a subclass override behaviour through a small table of pointers. Operator overloading lets new types, such as complex numbers, use + and *.`,history:`In 1979 Bjarne Stroustrup, a Danish computer scientist who had used Simula for his doctorate at Cambridge, joined Bell Labs and began 'C with Classes'. In 1983 the language was renamed C++, a name suggested by Rick Mascitti after C's ++ operator, which adds one. In 1985 AT&T released Cfront, a compiler that translated C++ into C, together with Stroustrup's book The C++ Programming Language. In 1989 Cfront 2.0 added multiple inheritance. In 1990 Borland's Turbo C++ brought the language to PC programmers. In 1998 ISO published the first C++ standard, including Alexander Stepanov's Standard Template Library.`,facts:[`Cfront of 1985 translated C++ into C, so any machine with a C compiler could use it.`,`The first international C++ standard was published in 1998.`]},"tech:gcc":{intro:`A free compiler toolchain is a complete set of tools for turning source code into running programs that anyone may use, copy and change. It is portable: one design generates good code for many different processors. Universities, hobbyists and makers of new chips no longer have to buy an expensive compiler or write their own.`,how:`GCC reads C, turns it into a tree, then into an intermediate language called RTL, register transfer language, which describes simple operations on registers. A machine description file for each processor tells the back end how RTL maps onto that chip's instructions. Adding a new processor means writing a description, not a new compiler, and every optimisation is shared by all the targets.`,history:`In 1985 Richard Stallman began work on a GNU C compiler after attempts to adapt existing compilers failed. In March 1987 Stallman released GCC 1.0, the GNU C Compiler. In December 1987 Michael Tiemann extended it to C++. In 1989 Tiemann co-founded Cygnus Solutions, one of the first companies to make a business of supporting free software, much of it GCC. In 1991 Linus Torvalds built the first Linux kernel with GCC. In 1999 GCC was renamed the GNU Compiler Collection, as it now handled C, C++, Fortran and more.`,facts:[`Cygnus, founded in 1989, earned its living porting and supporting GCC for chip makers.`,`GCC became the GNU Compiler Collection in 1999.`]},"tech:clientserver":{intro:`A client-server database splits the work: a database server on its own machine stores the data and runs SQL, while desktop PCs, the clients, show forms and reports and send it queries over the office network. Only the questions and answers cross the wire, not whole files. Companies can move work off costly mainframes onto cheaper minicomputers and PCs.`,how:`The client program sends SQL to the server and gets back just the rows it needs. Business rules can live on the server as stored procedures, small programs that run beside the data, and triggers that fire when rows change, so every client follows the same rules. Desktop tools let developers draw forms that talk to the server with little code.`,history:`In 1984 Mark Hoffman and Bob Epstein founded Sybase in Berkeley, California, to build a database designed from the start for networks. In 1987 Sybase released SQL Server with stored procedures and triggers. In 1988 Microsoft, Ashton-Tate and Sybase agreed to bring it to IBM's OS/2. In 1991 Powersoft's PowerBuilder made it easy to draw client programs for such servers. In 1992 SAP launched R/3, its business software built on client-server lines. In 1993 Microsoft released its own SQL Server for Windows NT, and in 1994 the partnership with Sybase ended.`,facts:[`Sybase was founded in 1984 by Mark Hoffman and Bob Epstein.`,`SAP's R/3 of 1992 moved big-company accounting onto client-server machines.`]},"tech:scripting":{intro:`A scripting language is built for quick, practical jobs: gluing programs together, reshaping text files and producing reports. Programs run straight from source with no separate compile step, variables need no declarations, and strings, lists and lookup tables are built in. A task that takes a page of C can take a few lines.`,how:`An interpreter compiles the script into an internal form each time it starts and runs it at once. Memory is managed automatically, and strings grow as needed. Perl borrowed powerful regular expressions from Unix tools such as sed and awk for searching and rewriting text, and hashes for looking things up by name. Scripts can call any other program and capture its output.`,history:`In 1977 Alfred Aho, Peter Weinberger and Brian Kernighan at Bell Labs created awk, a small language for processing text files line by line. In December 1987 Larry Wall, a programmer at Unisys, posted Perl 1.0 to the Usenet newsgroups as free source code. In 1988 John Ousterhout at Berkeley created Tcl, a language for embedding in other programs. In 1991 Guido van Rossum at the CWI research centre in Amsterdam released Python. In 1994 Perl 5 added modules and objects. In 1995 Yukihiro Matsumoto released Ruby in Japan.`,facts:[`Perl was nearly called Pearl, but Wall found that name taken in 1987.`,`Python, released in 1991, was named after the comedy series Monty Python's Flying Circus, not the snake.`]},"tech:diskcompress":{intro:`Transparent disk compression squeezes every file as it is written to disk and unsqueezes it as it is read, without users or programs noticing. A hard disk appears to hold about twice as much. With PC hard disks small and expensive, it offers extra capacity for the price of a program.`,how:`The software creates one huge hidden file on the real disk and presents it to the operating system as a new drive. A driver sits between DOS and the disk, compressing each cluster with a fast LZ method on the way in and decompressing on the way out. On a fast processor this costs little, and since fewer bytes travel to and from a slow disk, it can even seem quicker.`,history:`In 1990 Stac Electronics of San Diego, which had made compression chips, shipped Stacker, which could run in software or with an optional add-in card. In 1991 Digital Research's DR DOS 6.0 included SuperStor disk compression. In 1993 Microsoft built DoubleSpace into MS-DOS 6.0 after talks to license Stacker failed. In 1994 a jury found that Microsoft had infringed Stac's patents. In 1994 the firms settled, Microsoft paying licence fees and buying a stake in Stac, and MS-DOS 6.22 switched to a rewritten DriveSpace. By 1998 hard disks had become so cheap that few people bothered.`,facts:[`A typical new PC hard disk held about 40 MB in 1990.`,`MS-DOS 6.22 of 1994 replaced DoubleSpace with DriveSpace.`]},"tech:cvs":{intro:`CVS, the Concurrent Versions System, lets a team keep a project in one shared repository while each programmer works on a private copy at the same time. Nobody locks files: when two people change the same file, the tool merges their edits and flags only real conflicts. Whole projects, not just single files, can be tagged and fetched by release.`,how:`A developer checks out a working copy, edits freely, then runs update to merge in other people's changes and commit to send their own. Underneath, CVS stores each file's history in RCS format on the server. When two edits touch the same lines, CVS marks the clash in the file for a person to settle. Branches let a released version get bug fixes while new work continues on the main line.`,history:`In 1986 Dick Grune at the Vrije Universiteit in Amsterdam wrote CVS as shell scripts on top of RCS, so he and his students could work on a compiler at the same time. In 1989 Brian Berliner rewrote it in C, and in 1990 the C version was released freely. In the 1990s its network mode let programmers anywhere on the internet work on shared projects, and most free software was kept in CVS. In 2000 the company CollabNet started Subversion to fix its flaws, such as being unable to rename files or commit several files as one change. In 2010 Subversion became a project of the Apache Software Foundation.`,facts:[`By 2002 SourceForge gave free CVS hosting to tens of thousands of projects.`,`Subversion reached version 1.0 in 2004.`]},"tech:linux":{intro:`Linux is a free Unix-like operating system kernel written by a Finnish computer science student for his own 386 PC and shared over the internet with full source code. Combined with free tools from the GNU project, it makes a complete system anyone can copy. Programmers around the world send in fixes and features, and its author decides what goes in.`,how:`The kernel manages memory, processes, files and devices, and offers the same system calls as Unix, so existing Unix programs can be recompiled to run on it. It was written in C and built with GCC, using the 386 chip's protected mode and memory paging. Volunteers send patches by email and newsgroup; the best are merged into the next release, which comes out every few weeks.`,history:`In 1987 Andrew Tanenbaum's teaching system MINIX showed students how a Unix-like kernel works, and it was the system Linus Torvalds used. In August 1991 Torvalds, a 21-year-old student at the University of Helsinki, posted to the comp.os.minix newsgroup that he was writing a free operating system. In January 1992 Tanenbaum's post 'LINUX is obsolete' started a famous debate about kernel design. In 1992 Torvalds put the kernel under the GNU GPL, and the first distributions bundled it with GNU tools. In March 1994 version 1.0 was released. In 1996 the penguin Tux was chosen as its mascot.`,facts:[`Linux 1.0 of 1994 had about 176,000 lines of code.`,`Torvalds was 21 when he announced Linux in 1991.`]},"tech:visualbuilder":{intro:`A visual GUI builder lets a programmer draw a program's window instead of coding it: drag buttons, text boxes and lists from a palette onto a form, set their colours and captions, and double-click a button to write the code that runs when it is pressed. Building a Windows program becomes something an office worker can attempt.`,how:`Each control is an object with properties, such as caption and size, and events, such as Click. The builder writes the layout code itself and leaves the programmer to fill in small event handlers. Visual Basic runs these in an interpreter, so changes can be tried at once. Other firms sell extra controls, such as grids and charts, that drop into the palette like the built-in ones.`,history:`In 1988 NeXT's Interface Builder let developers lay out windows and wire them to code by dragging. In 1988 Alan Cooper showed Bill Gates a prototype called Tripod, in which users built tools by dragging controls onto a screen, and Microsoft bought it. In May 1991 Microsoft joined Cooper's design to its QuickBASIC language and released Visual Basic 1.0. In 1993 Visual Basic 3.0 added easy database access and became one of the most used programming tools in business. In 1995 Borland answered with Delphi, which compiled to fast native code.`,facts:[`Alan Cooper sold his Tripod prototype to Microsoft in 1988.`,`Visual Basic 3.0 of 1993 added database access through the Jet engine.`]},"tech:warehouse":{intro:`A data warehouse is a separate, huge database that gathers copies of a company's records from all its operational systems: sales, stock and customers. It is organised for asking questions across years of history rather than for taking orders quickly. OLAP, online analytical processing, lets managers slice the figures by product, region and month and drill down into the detail.`,how:`Each night, extract-transform-load jobs copy the day's records from the order systems, clean them and fit them to common codes. Data is often arranged as a star: a central fact table of sales surrounded by dimension tables for time, product and store. OLAP tools precompute totals in a cube, so a question like sales of tea in the north by quarter comes back in seconds.`,history:`In 1984 Teradata of California shipped its DBC/1012, a database machine that spread one database across many processors. In 1988 Barry Devlin and Paul Murphy of IBM Ireland described a 'business data warehouse' in the IBM Systems Journal. In 1992 Bill Inmon's book Building the Data Warehouse popularised the idea, and Teradata built Walmart a warehouse larger than a terabyte. In 1993 Edgar Codd's report defining OLAP was sponsored by Arbor Software, maker of the Essbase OLAP server, which raised eyebrows. In 1996 Ralph Kimball's The Data Warehouse Toolkit made the star schema the standard design.`,facts:[`In 1992 Teradata built Walmart the first commercial database larger than 1 terabyte.`,`Kimball's 1996 book made the star schema the standard warehouse design.`]},"tech:jpeg":{intro:`JPEG is a standard for compressing photographs, named after the Joint Photographic Experts Group of international standards bodies that wrote it. It can shrink a colour photo to a tenth of its size or less with little visible loss, and the user chooses the trade-off between quality and size. Pictures become small enough to store by the thousand and send over a modem.`,how:`The image is first converted from red, green and blue into brightness and two colour channels, and the colour channels are often halved in resolution, since the eye is less sharp for colour. Each 8×8 block is transformed with the DCT; its coefficients are divided by a quantisation table and rounded, read out in a zigzag from coarse to fine, and finally Huffman coded.`,history:`In 1986 the ISO and CCITT standards bodies formed the Joint Photographic Experts Group to agree one method for still pictures. In 1988 the committee tested competing proposals and chose an adaptive DCT method. In 1991 the Independent JPEG Group, led by Tom Lane, released free code that anyone could build into their programs. In 1992 the standard was published as ITU-T T.81, and Eric Hamilton's JFIF file format gave JPEG pictures a common wrapper. In 1994 the Netscape browser showed JPEG photos inside web pages.`,facts:[`A JPEG photo of 1992 typically needed a tenth of the space of the raw image, or less.`,`The Independent JPEG Group released its free code in 1991, before the standard was even final.`]},"tech:deflate":{intro:`DEFLATE is a compression method that combines LZ77-style copying of repeated strings with Huffman coding of what remains. It is fast, compresses well on almost any data, and its format is openly documented and free of patents. One program can unpack what another packed, on any computer.`,how:`The compressor searches a 32 KB window for the longest earlier match of the bytes coming up and outputs either a literal byte or a length and a distance. These symbols are then Huffman coded, with code tables built for each block of data and sent compactly at the start of the block. Hash tables of three-byte strings make the search for matches quick.`,history:`In 1991 Phil Katz began designing DEFLATE for version 2 of his PKZIP archiver. In October 1992 Jean-loup Gailly and Mark Adler released gzip for the GNU project, using the same method as a patent-free replacement for the Unix compress program, whose LZW was patented. In 1993 PKZIP 2 made DEFLATE the standard method inside ZIP files. In 1995 Gailly and Adler released zlib, a free library that let any program use DEFLATE. In 1996 the Internet Engineering Task Force published DEFLATE as RFC 1951, written by Peter Deutsch. In 1997 the HTTP/1.1 standard let web servers send pages compressed with gzip.`,facts:[`DEFLATE can copy a repeat from up to 32 KB back (1993).`,`Zlib, released in 1995, was free for any use, including commercial products.`]},"tech:protectedos":{intro:`A protected 32-bit operating system gives each program its own private memory space, guarded by the processor, so a crash in one program cannot corrupt another or bring down the whole computer. It uses the full 32-bit power of chips like Intel's 386, with flat memory of up to four gigabytes, and switches between programs itself instead of waiting for each to give up control.`,how:`The processor runs the kernel in a privileged mode and applications in a restricted one; page tables give each process its own view of memory, and touching an address outside it raises a fault the system can handle. A timer interrupt lets the scheduler take the processor away from any thread. Windows NT's kernel was designed to be portable, with a thin hardware layer, and ran on Intel, MIPS and Alpha chips.`,history:`In 1988 Microsoft hired Dave Cutler, chief architect of DEC's VMS, to build a new operating system from scratch after DEC cancelled his latest project. In 1992 IBM shipped OS/2 2.0, a 32-bit protected system for 386 PCs, after splitting with Microsoft. In July 1993 Windows NT 3.1 shipped with a new file system, NTFS. In 1995 Windows 95 brought 32-bit programs and preemptive multitasking to ordinary PCs, though it kept parts of DOS underneath. In 2001 Windows XP moved home users onto the NT kernel, and Apple's Mac OS X gave the Mac protected memory the same year.`,facts:[`Windows NT 3.1 needed at least 12 MB of memory in 1993, when many PCs had 4 MB.`,`Cutler's team began work on Windows NT in late 1988.`]},"tech:mpegvideo":{intro:`MPEG video coding compresses moving pictures by sending a complete picture only now and then and, for the frames in between, mostly describing how blocks of the picture have moved. It shrinks video by a factor of around a hundred, small enough to fit a film on compact discs or several TV channels into the space of one analogue channel.`,how:`I-frames are coded on their own, like JPEG pictures. P-frames are predicted from an earlier frame: for each 16×16 macroblock the encoder searches for the best-matching area and sends a motion vector plus a DCT-coded correction. B-frames are predicted from frames both before and after. The encoder does the hard searching; decoders are simpler and cheap enough for consumer chips.`,history:`In 1988 Leonardo Chiariglione and Hiroshi Yasuda set up the Moving Picture Experts Group under the ISO. In 1993 MPEG-1 was published, aimed at about 1.5 megabits a second, the data rate of a CD, and Video CDs using it appeared the same year. In 1995 MPEG-2 followed for broadcast-quality television, with support for interlaced TV pictures. In 1996 the DVD, carrying MPEG-2 films, went on sale in Japan. By 1998 satellite, cable and the first digital terrestrial TV services in Britain and America broadcast in MPEG-2.`,facts:[`MPEG-1 fitted VHS-quality video and stereo sound into about 1.5 megabits a second (1993).`,`A Video CD held about 74 minutes of MPEG-1 video (1993).`,`DVDs, from 1996, carried MPEG-2 video at up to about 10 megabits a second.`]},"tech:mp3":{intro:`Perceptual audio coding shrinks recorded sound by throwing away what human ears cannot hear. Quiet sounds close in pitch to loud ones are masked and need not be stored, and the rest is coded only as precisely as hearing requires. Music can be cut to about a tenth of its CD size and still sound nearly the same to most listeners.`,how:`The encoder splits the sound into short frames and many frequency bands, using a filter bank and a modified DCT. A psychoacoustic model, built from experiments on listeners, estimates how much noise each band can hide at that moment. The encoder then quantises each band just coarsely enough to keep the noise below that threshold, and Huffman codes the result.`,history:`In 1982 Karlheinz Brandenburg began a doctorate at the University of Erlangen-Nuremberg on coding music to send over digital phone lines. In the late 1980s his team at the Fraunhofer Institute tuned their coder by listening again and again to Suzanne Vega's unaccompanied song Tom's Diner. In 1993 the method was published as MPEG-1 Audio Layer III. In 1997 the free Winamp player made playing MP3 files on a PC easy. In 1998 Saehan's MPMan, one of the first portable MP3 players, went on sale in Korea. In 1999 Napster let millions of people swap MP3 files over the internet.`,facts:[`A 128 kbit/s MP3 is about one-eleventh the size of CD audio (1993).`,`Napster, launched in 1999, had tens of millions of users by 2001.`]},"tech:bwt":{intro:`Block-sorting compression, built on the Burrows-Wheeler transform, rearranges a block of text so that identical letters end up next to each other, then compresses the result. The rearrangement can be undone exactly. It packs text noticeably tighter than LZ-style compressors, at the cost of more time and memory.`,how:`Take a block, list every rotation of it and sort them alphabetically; output the last letter of each sorted rotation. Letters that come before the same context, such as the letter before 'he' in English, cluster together. A move-to-front step turns those clusters into runs of small numbers, which Huffman or arithmetic coding then squeezes well. Knowing which row was the original, the decoder reverses everything.`,history:`In 1983 David Wheeler, the EDSAC pioneer, discovered the transform but did not publish it. In May 1994 Michael Burrows and Wheeler published it as a research report at DEC's Systems Research Center in Palo Alto. In 1996 Julian Seward released bzip, which used arithmetic coding, and then bzip2, which switched to Huffman coding to avoid patents. By 2000 many Unix and Linux sites offered downloads in bzip2 format alongside gzip.`,facts:[`bzip2 files of 1996 were often 10 to 20 per cent smaller than gzip files of the same text.`,`Wheeler, who found the transform in 1983, had helped program EDSAC in 1949.`]},"tech:java":{intro:`Java is an object-oriented language with a C-like look, compiled not to machine code but to bytecode for the Java Virtual Machine, so the same compiled program runs on any computer with a JVM. Memory is reclaimed automatically by a garbage collector, array bounds are checked, and programs downloaded from the web run inside a security sandbox.`,how:`The javac compiler turns source files into class files of compact bytecode. The virtual machine checks each class before loading it, refusing code that would break type safety, then runs it. A large standard library supplies networking, threads, data structures and windows, so programs depend less on the operating system underneath. Small programs called applets can be embedded in web pages and run in the browser.`,history:`In 1991 James Gosling's Green team at Sun Microsystems began a language called Oak for programming television set-top boxes and other devices. In 1994 the team saw that the web needed portable, safe code, and built a browser, HotJava, that could run small programs called applets. In 1995 Oak, renamed Java because of a trademark clash, was announced with Netscape's support. In 1999 Sun's HotSpot virtual machine compiled busy code to native speed while it ran. In 2006 Sun began releasing Java as open source under the GPL. In 2010 Oracle bought Sun, and Java with it.`,facts:[`Sun's 1995 slogan for Java was 'Write once, run anywhere'.`,`Oracle bought Sun Microsystems for about $7.4 billion in 2010.`]},"tech:osdb":{intro:`Open-source SQL databases are relational database systems whose code is free to download, use and change. They handle the everyday work of storing users, posts and orders well enough for most websites and small businesses, without licence fees for each processor. Small teams can run a real database on a cheap PC server.`,how:`A server process listens for connections, parses SQL and stores tables in files on disk, with B-tree indexes for fast lookups. Early versions traded features for speed and simplicity: MySQL at first left out transactions and foreign keys to stay fast on simple reads. Having the source means anyone can fix bugs, move the server to a new system or add features.`,history:`In 1986 Michael Stonebraker's team at Berkeley began Postgres, a successor to Ingres. In 1994 two students, Andrew Yu and Jolly Chen, added SQL to Postgres and released it as Postgres95. In 1995 Michael Widenius and David Axmark released MySQL in Sweden, under terms free for most uses. In 1996 Postgres95 was renamed PostgreSQL and a volunteer team took it over. In 2000 MySQL moved to the GPL, and Richard Hipp released SQLite, a whole database in one small library, first written for a US Navy project. In 2008 Sun Microsystems bought MySQL AB for about $1 billion.`,facts:[`MySQL was named after My, the daughter of co-founder Michael Widenius (1995).`,`Sun Microsystems bought MySQL AB for about $1 billion in 2008.`]},"tech:webscript":{intro:`Server-side web scripting lets a web server build each page fresh when someone asks for it, by running small programs mixed into the page's HTML. A page can show the latest stock levels, a visitor's basket or the latest forum posts straight from a database. Making a dynamic website no longer needs a separate compiled program for every page.`,how:`Special tags mark the code inside an HTML file. When a request arrives, the server runs that code, which may read form fields, query a database and print HTML, and sends the visitor only the finished page. Because the code sits inside the page, a designer can edit the layout and a programmer the logic in the same file.`,history:`In 1993 the Common Gateway Interface let web servers such as NCSA's run an outside program, often a Perl script, to produce a page. In June 1995 Rasmus Lerdorf, a Danish-Canadian programmer, released Personal Home Page Tools, which he had written to track visits to his online CV. In July 1995 Allaire's ColdFusion offered a similar tag-based approach for business websites. In December 1996 Microsoft introduced Active Server Pages with its IIS web server. In 1998 PHP 3, rewritten by Andi Gutmans and Zeev Suraski, made PHP a full language. In 2004 Facebook was launched as a PHP program.`,facts:[`PHP 3 of 1998 was rewritten by two students, Andi Gutmans and Zeev Suraski.`,`Facebook launched in 2004 as a PHP website.`]},"tech:javascript":{intro:`JavaScript is a scripting language that runs inside the web browser, letting a page react to the user without asking the server: check a form before it is sent, change an image when the mouse passes over it, open a window. Its syntax looks like C and Java, but it is loosely typed and forgiving, aimed at designers and amateurs as much as professional programmers.`,how:`Scripts sit inside the page's HTML and are run by the browser as the page loads. They can read and change parts of the page through objects such as document and window, and respond to events like onClick. Functions are values that can be passed around, and objects are flexible bags of properties that can grow at any time, ideas borrowed from the languages Scheme and Self.`,history:`In May 1995 Brendan Eich at Netscape wrote the first version, under the name Mocha. In December 1995 it was renamed JavaScript, in a marketing deal with Sun, whose Java was the talk of the industry. In March 1996 Netscape Navigator 2.0 shipped as the first browser to run it. In August 1996 Microsoft shipped its own version, JScript, in Internet Explorer 3. In 1997 Ecma International published the language as a standard called ECMAScript. In 2005 Jesse James Garrett coined the term Ajax for pages, like Google Maps, that used JavaScript to fetch data in the background.`,facts:[`Netscape Navigator 2.0, the first browser with JavaScript, shipped in 1996.`,`ECMAScript, the standard version of JavaScript, was first published in 1997.`]},"tech:png":{intro:`PNG, Portable Network Graphics, is a free, lossless format for pictures such as diagrams, screenshots, icons and logos. It compresses better than GIF, supports millions of colours and allows smooth transparency, so an image can blend into any background. Its compression uses no patented method, so anyone may write software for it without paying.`,how:`Before compressing, each row of pixels passes through a filter that replaces each value with its difference from a neighbour, turning smooth gradients into runs of small numbers. DEFLATE then squeezes the result. The file is a series of labelled chunks, each with a CRC check, so new kinds of information can be added and older readers simply skip them. Gamma information helps an image look the same on different screens.`,history:`In December 1994 Unisys and CompuServe announced that programs writing GIF images would owe royalties on the LZW patent. In January 1995 Thomas Boutell posted a call on Usenet for a free replacement, and volunteers designed the new format over email within months. In October 1996 the World Wide Web Consortium published PNG as a Recommendation. In March 1997 it also became the internet standard RFC 2083. In 2004 PNG became an ISO standard. In 2006 Internet Explorer 7 finally displayed PNG transparency properly, removing the last big obstacle to its use on the web.`,facts:[`PNG supports up to 48 bits of colour per pixel, plus transparency (1996).`,`Internet Explorer handled PNG transparency properly only from version 7, in 2006.`]},"tech:unittest":{intro:`A unit-test framework is a small library that makes it easy to write automated tests for each piece of a program and run them all with one command. Each test sets up an example, calls the code and checks the result against what was expected. A green bar means everything passes; a red bar says exactly which test broke.`,how:`Programmers write test classes alongside their code, with methods that call assertion functions such as assertEquals. The framework finds every test, runs each one in isolation with fresh setup, catches failures and errors, and prints a report. Because the tests run in seconds, developers run them after every change, so a mistake shows up minutes after it was made rather than months later at a customer's site.`,history:`In 1994 Kent Beck wrote SUnit, a simple testing framework for Smalltalk. In 1997, on a flight from Zurich to Atlanta for the OOPSLA conference, Beck and Erich Gamma wrote JUnit, a version for Java. In 1999 Beck's book Extreme Programming Explained made writing tests a core part of everyday programming. By 2002 copies of the design, known as the xUnit family, existed for most languages, from Python's PyUnit to NUnit for Microsoft's .NET. In 2002 Beck's book Test-Driven Development: By Example urged programmers to write each test before the code it checks.`,facts:[`JUnit was written in 1997 during a single flight from Zurich to Atlanta.`,`Beck's 2002 book made test-driven development, writing the test first, widely known.`]},"tech:uml":{intro:`UML, the Unified Modeling Language, is a standard set of diagrams for designing object-oriented software: class diagrams for the parts and how they relate, sequence diagrams for messages between objects, use-case diagrams for what users want, and more. One shared notation replaces dozens of competing ones, so designs can be read by any team and handled by any tool.`,how:`Classes are boxes listing their attributes and operations, joined by lines for associations and arrows for inheritance. State machines show how an object reacts to events, and activity diagrams show workflows. Modelling tools such as Rational Rose store the diagrams in a model, check them and generate code skeletons in C++ or Java; the dream of model-driven design is to generate much more of the program from the model.`,history:`In the early 1990s dozens of object-oriented design methods competed, the best known by Grady Booch, by James Rumbaugh and by Ivar Jacobson. In 1994 Rumbaugh joined Booch at Rational Software, and in 1995 Rational bought Jacobson's company Objectory, uniting the three men, nicknamed the Three Amigos. In November 1997 the Object Management Group adopted UML 1.1 as a standard. In 2001 the OMG launched Model Driven Architecture, aiming to generate whole systems from models. In 2003 IBM bought Rational. In 2005 UML 2.0 was adopted, much larger than the first version.`,facts:[`IBM bought Rational Software for $2.1 billion in 2003.`,`UML 2.0, adopted in 2005, defined 13 kinds of diagram.`]},"tech:opensource":{intro:`Open-source development is a way of building software in public: the code is freely available, anyone can study it and send improvements, and a project grows through a community of volunteers and companies rather than one firm's closed team. Its supporters argue it produces more reliable software because, as they put it, with enough eyes all bugs are shallow.`,how:`The source lives in a public repository, discussion happens on mailing lists, and anyone can send a patch. A small group of maintainers reviews changes and decides what goes in. Licences such as the GPL or the BSD and MIT terms allow free use and change. Companies join because sharing the cost of common software, like a web server or an operating system, is cheaper than each building their own.`,history:`In 1995 a group of webmasters patched the abandoned NCSA web server into Apache, a project run entirely in the open. In 1997 Eric Raymond's essay The Cathedral and the Bazaar contrasted closed, planned development with Linux's open, noisy bazaar. In January 1998 Netscape announced that it would release the source code of its browser, partly inspired by the essay. In February 1998 Christine Peterson suggested the term open source, and Raymond and Bruce Perens founded the Open Source Initiative to promote it. In June 1998 IBM announced it would ship and support Apache. In 1999 the Apache developers formed the Apache Software Foundation.`,facts:[`Netscape published its browser source code, as Mozilla, on 31 March 1998.`,`The Apache Software Foundation was formed in 1999.`]},"tech:bugtracker":{intro:`An issue tracker is a shared database of bugs and tasks, usually reached through a web browser. Each issue gets a number and a page recording who reported it, how to reproduce it, how serious it is, who is fixing it and what state it is in. Nothing gets lost in someone's inbox, and anyone can see what is being worked on.`,how:`Issues move through a workflow: new, assigned, fixed, verified, closed. Reporters attach files and the steps to reproduce a problem; developers comment, link duplicates and mark which version fixes it. Searches and saved queries give managers lists such as every critical bug open for the next release, and emails tell people when an issue they follow changes.`,history:`In 1992 the GNU project's GNATS let free-software teams track bugs reported by email. In 1998 Terry Weissman at Netscape wrote Bugzilla to replace the company's in-house tracker when the Mozilla project opened to outside developers. In 2002 Atlassian, founded in Sydney by Mike Cannon-Brookes and Scott Farquhar, released Jira for companies' software teams. In 2009 GitHub added simple built-in issues to every project it hosted. In 2015 Atlassian floated on the Nasdaq stock exchange.`,facts:[`Atlassian's founders started it in 2002 with about A$10,000 of credit-card debt.`,`Jira's name of 2002 is a shortening of Gojira, Japanese for Godzilla, a nod to Bugzilla.`]},"tech:consensus":{intro:`A consensus protocol lets a group of computers agree on a single value, such as the next change to a database or who is in charge, even when some of them crash or messages are lost or delayed. As long as a majority are working and can talk, they reach the same decision and never contradict each other. It is a foundation for building reliable systems from unreliable machines.`,how:`In Paxos, a proposer picks a ballot number and asks the acceptors to promise to ignore older ballots. If a majority promise, it proposes a value, adopting any value an acceptor has already accepted, and the value is chosen once a majority accept it. Because any two majorities share at least one machine, two different values can never both win. Repeating this for each slot builds a replicated log.`,history:`In 1982 Leslie Lamport, Robert Shostak and Marshall Pease described the Byzantine Generals Problem, where some machines may even lie. In 1985 Michael Fischer, Nancy Lynch and Michael Paterson proved that no protocol can guarantee agreement in every case if messages can be delayed and even one machine can fail. In 1989 Lamport wrote up Paxos as the story of a fictional Greek island parliament, but reviewers found the joke distracting and it went unpublished. In 1998 it finally appeared as 'The Part-Time Parliament' in ACM Transactions on Computer Systems. In 2001 Lamport published a plainer version, 'Paxos Made Simple'. In 2014 Diego Ongaro and John Ousterhout at Stanford published Raft, designed to be easier to understand.`,facts:[`A five-machine Paxos group keeps working with any two machines failed (1998).`,`Leslie Lamport received the 2013 Turing Award, partly for Paxos.`]},"tech:lamp":{intro:`The LAMP stack is a combination of four free programs that together run a dynamic website: the Linux operating system, the Apache web server, the MySQL database and the PHP scripting language. Each is free to download and runs on a cheap PC server, so a student or small business can build a site with forums, shops or news for the cost of the hardware.`,how:`Apache receives a browser's request and hands pages ending in .php to the PHP module running inside it. The PHP code connects to MySQL, runs SQL queries and prints HTML with the results, which Apache sends back. Linux keeps it all running and is managed remotely over a text console. Hosting firms pack hundreds of such sites onto one machine.`,history:`In December 1998 Michael Kunze, writing in the German computer magazine c't, used the name LAMP for this free set of tools and argued it could rival expensive commercial packages. In 2000 MySQL's move to the GPL made the whole stack open source. In 2002 Wikipedia moved to MediaWiki, a PHP and MySQL program. In 2003 Matt Mullenweg and Mike Little released WordPress, a blogging tool written in PHP for MySQL. In 2004 Facebook started on the same stack in a Harvard dormitory.`,facts:[`WordPress, a LAMP program first released in 2003, ran about 40% of websites by 2021.`,`Kunze's 1998 article appeared in c't, one of Germany's leading computer magazines.`]},"tech:lzma":{intro:`LZMA, the Lempel-Ziv-Markov chain algorithm, is a compression method that finds repeats over a very large window, many megabytes long, and codes the result with an adaptive range coder that predicts each bit from what came before. It packs software and documents much tighter than ZIP. Compressing is slow and hungry for memory, but unpacking is fast.`,how:`The match finder searches a large dictionary window for long repeats and also remembers the last few match distances, which are cheap to reuse. Each decision, literal or match, short or long, is coded bit by bit with a range coder, a form of arithmetic coding, using probabilities chosen by context and updated constantly. Good predictions mean many decisions cost only a small fraction of a bit.`,history:`In 1999 Igor Pavlov, a Russian programmer, released the free archiver 7-Zip, whose own 7z format used his LZMA method. In 2008 Pavlov placed his LZMA code in the public domain, so anyone could build it into their products. In 2009 xz Utils brought LZMA to Unix and Linux as the .xz format. In 2013 kernel.org stopped offering bzip2 downloads of the Linux source in favour of xz. In 2024 a backdoor was found hidden in xz Utils, slipped in by a contributor who had gained the project's trust over two years.`,facts:[`The 2024 xz backdoor was caught by Andres Freund, who noticed logins taking half a second too long.`,`7-Zip has been free to use since its first release in 1999.`]},"tech:x86virt":{intro:`PC-server virtualisation lets one ordinary Intel-based computer run several complete operating systems at once, each in its own virtual machine with virtual disks, network cards and screen. One physical server can replace many underused ones, and a virtual machine can be copied, backed up or moved like a file.`,how:`The x86 processor cannot be virtualised by simple trapping: some sensitive instructions quietly behave differently in user mode instead of trapping. VMware's monitor runs ordinary guest code directly, but scans the guest kernel's code as it runs and rewrites the troublesome instructions into safe sequences, a method called binary translation. Virtual devices imitate common real hardware, so standard drivers work unchanged.`,history:`In 1998 Mendel Rosenblum, Diane Greene, Scott Devine, Edward Wang and Edouard Bugnion founded VMware in Palo Alto, building on research at Stanford. In May 1999 VMware Workstation let developers run Linux and Windows side by side on one PC. In 2001 ESX Server ran directly on the hardware, without a host operating system underneath. In 2005 Intel added hardware support for virtualisation, called VT-x, to its processors, and AMD followed in 2006. In 2007 VMware floated on the stock market. In 2023 the chip company Broadcom completed its purchase of VMware.`,facts:[`Broadcom completed its purchase of VMware for about $69 billion in 2023.`,`Intel added VT-x hardware virtualisation to its processors in 2005.`]},"tech:agile":{intro:`Agile methods are ways of running software projects in short cycles of one to four weeks, each ending with working software shown to the customer. Plans change as the team and customer learn, instead of following a long specification written up front. Small teams work closely together, talk face to face, and value working code over thick documents.`,how:`In Scrum, a product owner keeps a ranked list of wanted features; each sprint the team commits to a slice, meets daily for a short stand-up, and ends with a review and a retrospective. Extreme Programming adds engineering practices: writing tests first, pairing two programmers at one keyboard, integrating many times a day and keeping the design simple. Progress is measured by features finished, not documents written.`,history:`In 1995 Ken Schwaber and Jeff Sutherland presented Scrum at the OOPSLA conference. In 1996 Kent Beck began Extreme Programming on Chrysler's C3 payroll project. In February 2001 seventeen developers met at the Snowbird ski resort in Utah and wrote the Manifesto for Agile Software Development. The 2001 manifesto put individuals over processes, working software over documentation, customer collaboration over contracts and responding to change over following a plan. In 2010 Schwaber and Sutherland published the first Scrum Guide.`,facts:[`Chrysler's C3 payroll project, where XP began in 1996, was cancelled in 2000.`,`The 2020 Scrum Guide is under 20 pages long.`]},"tech:ci":{intro:`Continuous integration means every developer merges their changes into the shared code at least daily, and an automated server builds the whole product and runs its tests after each change. If the build breaks, the team hears within minutes and fixes it at once, while the change is small and fresh in mind, instead of facing a painful integration phase at the end of a project.`,how:`A build server watches the version control system. When a change arrives it checks out the code, compiles it, runs the unit tests and reports the result on a web page, by email or even with a lamp in the office. A broken build is the whole team's top priority. Fast tests and a single command that builds everything are what make it work.`,history:`In 1996 Steve McConnell described the daily build and smoke test used at Microsoft. In 1999 Kent Beck's Extreme Programming made integrating several times a day a core practice. In 2000 Martin Fowler and Matthew Foemmel of ThoughtWorks published an article called Continuous Integration, and in 2001 ThoughtWorks released CruiseControl. In 2005 Kohsuke Kawaguchi at Sun wrote Hudson, a build server teams could set up in minutes. In 2011 Travis CI offered free builds for open-source projects on GitHub. In 2019 GitHub launched Actions, building automation into the site itself.`,facts:[`Fowler and Foemmel's 2000 article made the term continuous integration widely known.`,`GitHub Actions, launched in 2019, ran builds free for public projects.`]},"tech:osssupport":{intro:`An open-source subscription is a business model in which the software itself is free to copy, but companies pay a yearly fee for a supported, tested and certified version, with security updates, legal protection and an expert to call. Big firms buy the promise that the system will be maintained for years and work with their hardware and databases.`,how:`The vendor takes the community's code, freezes a stable version, tests it with major hardware and software makers, and maintains it for many years with fixes. Customers pay per server per year. Certification by firms such as Oracle and SAP lets businesses run critical systems on it, and the vendor feeds its improvements back to the public projects.`,history:`In 1993 Marc Ewing created Red Hat Linux, and in 1995 his business merged with Bob Young's to form Red Hat Software in North Carolina. In August 1999 Red Hat's shares rose to several times their offer price on their first day of trading. In 2002 Red Hat launched an enterprise edition, Red Hat Enterprise Linux, sold only by subscription. In 2003 it stopped selling its boxed consumer version, handing that role to the free Fedora project. In 2003 Novell bought SUSE, the German Linux company. In 2004 Mark Shuttleworth's Canonical released Ubuntu, free to all, with paid support for businesses.`,facts:[`Novell bought SUSE for $210 million in 2003.`,`Canonical released the first Ubuntu, version 4.10, in October 2004.`]},"tech:avc":{intro:`H.264, also called Advanced Video Coding, is a video compression standard that delivers the same picture quality as MPEG-2 at about half the bit rate. It was written jointly by the telecoms and computing standards bodies for everything from mobile video calls to high-definition broadcasts. Many small improvements add up to its large gain.`,how:`Blocks can be as small as 4×4 pixels, and motion can be predicted from several earlier frames with quarter-pixel precision. Within a frame, blocks are predicted from their neighbours' edges. A whole-number transform replaces the DCT so encoder and decoder match exactly, a filter smooths block edges inside the loop, and CABAC, a context-adaptive arithmetic coder, squeezes the final bits.`,history:`In 1998 the ITU-T's Video Coding Experts Group started a project called H.26L to build a much better codec. In December 2001 ISO's MPEG group joined it to form the Joint Video Team. In May 2003 the standard was approved. In 2005 Apple's video iPod played H.264 files. In 2006 Blu-ray discs went on sale with H.264 among their video formats. In 2007 YouTube began re-encoding its videos in H.264 so they would play on Apple's iPhone.`,facts:[`MPEG LA's 2004 licence charged 20 cents a device after each maker's first 100,000 a year.`,`In 2007 YouTube began re-encoding its videos in H.264 for the iPhone.`]},"tech:paravirt":{intro:`Open hypervisors are free, open-source virtual machine monitors that anyone can use, study and build upon. Xen, the best known, uses paravirtualisation: the guest operating system is changed slightly to call the hypervisor directly instead of being tricked by it, so virtual machines run at close to full speed on ordinary PC servers.`,how:`Xen runs as a thin layer on the bare hardware. A privileged first guest, called dom0, runs Linux and handles the real device drivers; other guests send disk and network requests to it through rings of shared memory. Guest kernels replace privileged instructions with hypercalls, deliberate calls into the hypervisor, which avoids costly trapping and rewriting.`,history:`In October 2003 Ian Pratt's group at the University of Cambridge Computer Laboratory presented 'Xen and the Art of Virtualization' at the SOSP conference, showing Linux running in virtual machines with only a few per cent overhead. In 2006 Amazon's Elastic Compute Cloud launched on Xen. In 2006 Avi Kivity announced KVM, which turned the Linux kernel itself into a hypervisor using the new hardware support in Intel and AMD chips. In 2007 KVM was merged into Linux, and Citrix bought XenSource, the company behind Xen. In 2017 Amazon began moving its cloud to its own KVM-based Nitro system.`,facts:[`Citrix paid about $500 million for XenSource in 2007.`,`Amazon's cloud, launched in 2006, ran its virtual servers on Xen.`]},"tech:mapreduce":{intro:`Cluster computing on cheap machines spreads a huge job, such as indexing the whole web, across thousands of ordinary PCs. A distributed file system stores enormous files in pieces across their disks, and the MapReduce model lets a programmer write two simple functions while the system handles splitting, scheduling and failures. Machines break every day, so the software expects it.`,how:`GFS cuts files into 64 MB chunks and keeps three copies of each on different machines, with one master tracking where everything is. In MapReduce, map tasks read chunks and emit key-value pairs, such as a word and a count; the system sorts and groups them by key and hands each group to a reduce task that combines them. If a machine dies, its tasks simply run again elsewhere.`,history:`In October 2003 Sanjay Ghemawat, Howard Gobioff and Shun-Tak Leung of Google described the Google File System at the SOSP conference. In December 2004 Jeff Dean and Sanjay Ghemawat published MapReduce, explaining that Google had rewritten its web indexing with it. In 2005 Doug Cutting and Mike Cafarella copied the ideas into Nutch, an open-source search engine. In 2006 Yahoo hired Cutting, and the code became Hadoop, named after his son's toy elephant. In 2008 Yahoo ran its search index on a Hadoop cluster of more than 10,000 processor cores.`,facts:[`Google ran over 100,000 MapReduce jobs a day, processing 20 petabytes, by 2008.`,`In 2008 Hadoop sorted a terabyte in 209 seconds on 910 machines.`]},"tech:dvcs":{intro:`Distributed version control gives every developer a complete copy of a project's whole history, not just the latest files. Commits, branches and history browsing all work offline and fast, and changes are exchanged between copies as needed, so no central server is in charge. Branching and merging become cheap everyday acts instead of rare, feared events.`,how:`Git stores each version of each file as an object named by its SHA-1 hash, so identical content is stored once and any tampering would change the names. A commit points to a tree of files and to its parent commits, forming a history graph. Branches are just labels pointing at commits. Push and pull send only the objects the other side is missing.`,history:`In 2002 the Linux kernel began using BitKeeper, a commercial distributed system that Larry McVoy's BitMover offered free to open-source projects. In April 2005 BitMover withdrew the free licence after Andrew Tridgell worked out its network protocol, and Linus Torvalds began writing Git. In April 2005 Matt Mackall also started Mercurial for the same reason. In June 2005 Linux 2.6.12 became the first kernel release managed with Git, and in July Torvalds handed Git over to Junio Hamano. By 2018 nearly nine in ten developers in Stack Overflow's survey used Git.`,facts:[`Git was managing its own source code within days of its start in April 2005.`,`Junio Hamano has maintained Git since July 2005.`]},"tech:columnstore":{intro:`A column store keeps each column of a table together on disk instead of each row. A question like average price over a billion sales reads only the price column, not every field of every row. Values in one column are similar, so they compress very well, and the processor can crunch them in tight loops. It suits analysis, not taking orders.`,how:`Each column is stored as a long array, compressed with tricks such as run-length encoding, dictionaries of distinct values and storing small differences. The query engine works on compressed data where possible and processes a batch of values at a time. Rows are glued back together only at the end, and only for the columns the answer needs.`,history:`In 1985 George Copeland and Setrag Khoshafian proposed storing each column of a table separately, which they called a decomposition storage model. In the mid-1990s Sybase IQ sold column storage commercially for reporting. In 2005 Michael Stonebraker and colleagues from MIT, Brown and other universities presented C-Store and founded Vertica to sell it. In 2010 Google's Dremel paper described a column store for nested data that answered questions over huge tables in seconds. In 2011 HP bought Vertica. In 2013 Apache Parquet, based on Dremel's design, brought column files to Hadoop.`,facts:[`Google's 2010 Dremel paper described queries over trillion-row tables taking seconds.`,`HP bought Vertica, the company built on C-Store, in 2011.`]},"tech:mobileos":{intro:`A touch operating system is built from the start for a phone's glass screen and a fingertip, not for a keyboard, stylus or mouse. Scrolling, pinching and swiping feel physical, and almost the whole front of the phone becomes the display. Programs from other makers come through a single built-in shop, so adding one takes a couple of taps.`,how:`A capacitive screen senses several fingers at once, and the system turns their movements into gestures with momentum and bounce. Each app runs in its own sandbox with limited rights, so a bad one cannot harm the phone, and the system suspends apps in the background to save battery. The store is run by the platform owner, which reviews apps, handles payment and keeps a share of each sale.`,history:`In January 2007 Steve Jobs unveiled the iPhone, running a version of Mac OS X reworked for touch. In June 2007 it went on sale in the United States. In November 2007 Google, which had bought the start-up Android in 2005, announced Android together with an alliance of 34 companies. In July 2008 Apple opened the App Store, letting developers keep 70 per cent of each sale. In October 2008 the first Android phone, the T-Mobile G1 made by HTC, went on sale. By 2012 Android ran on more than half of all smartphones sold worldwide.`,facts:[`Apple's App Store opened in 2008 with about 500 apps.`,`The App Store passed one billion downloads in April 2009.`,`The first iPhone cost $499 with a two-year contract in 2007.`]},"tech:nosql":{intro:`Distributed NoSQL databases spread data across hundreds or thousands of ordinary servers and keep working when some fail. They give up parts of SQL, such as joins across tables or strict consistency, in return for scale, speed and constant availability. A shopping basket or a user's timeline can be stored and fetched in milliseconds, even under enormous load.`,how:`Keys are spread across machines by consistent hashing, a scheme in which adding a server moves only a small share of the data. Each item is copied to several machines, and a write succeeds when enough copies confirm it. Some systems let copies briefly disagree and settle differences afterwards using version stamps, which is called eventual consistency. Others store wide tables sorted by key, split into pieces served by many machines.`,history:`In November 2006 Google described Bigtable, the store behind its web indexing, Google Earth and Google Finance. In October 2007 Amazon's Dynamo paper explained how its shopping basket stayed writable even when machines and networks failed. In 2008 Facebook released Cassandra, built by Avinash Lakshman, one of Dynamo's authors, as open source. In June 2009 Johan Oskarsson used the tag NoSQL for a meeting in San Francisco about the new databases, and the name stuck. In 2009 MongoDB was released. In January 2012 Amazon offered DynamoDB as a cloud service.`,facts:[`The name NoSQL caught on after a 2009 meeting in San Francisco.`,`Amazon launched DynamoDB as a rentable cloud database in 2012.`]},"tech:containers":{intro:`A container is an isolated space inside one running operating system in which an application gets its own view of files, processes and network, and a fixed share of processor and memory. Unlike a virtual machine it needs no separate operating system kernel, so it starts in a fraction of a second, and dozens fit where a few virtual machines would.`,how:`On Linux, namespaces give each container its own process numbers, mount points, network interfaces and host name, so it cannot see its neighbours. Control groups, or cgroups, limit and measure how much processor time, memory and disk traffic each group may use. Together with a private root file system, these make a process look as if it had a machine of its own.`,history:`In 1979 Unix's chroot call let a process treat one directory as its whole file system, an early step towards isolation. In 2000 FreeBSD added jails, which walled off processes, files and network addresses. In 2006 Google engineers Paul Menage and Rohit Seth began work on process containers for Linux. In January 2008 their work was merged into Linux 2.6.24 as control groups. In 2008 the LXC project combined control groups and namespaces into usable Linux containers. In 2014 Google said everything it ran, from Search to Gmail, ran in containers.`,facts:[`In 2014 Google said it started more than 2 billion containers every week.`,`FreeBSD jails, an early form of container, arrived in 2000.`]},"tech:pullrequests":{intro:`A code-hosting site with pull requests turns open source into a social network. Anyone can fork a project, making their own copy with one click, change it and open a pull request asking the owners to merge the change back. The proposed change, its discussion and its review all sit on one web page beside the project's code.`,how:`A pull request shows the difference between the contributor's branch and the target branch, line by line. Reviewers comment on specific lines and ask for changes, and the contributor pushes fixes to the same branch. Profiles show each person's activity and public projects, so code becomes a portfolio employers can read.`,history:`In 2007 Tom Preston-Werner, Chris Wanstrath and PJ Hyett began building GitHub in San Francisco, and it launched in April 2008. In 2008 the Ruby on Rails project moved its code to GitHub, bringing many developers with it. In 2013 GitHub passed 10 million repositories. In 2015 Google announced it would close its rival Google Code, pointing projects towards GitHub. In 2018 Microsoft bought GitHub. In 2023 GitHub reported more than 100 million developers.`,facts:[`GitHub passed 10 million repositories in 2013.`,`GitHub reported more than 100 million developer accounts in 2023.`]},"tech:jitjs":{intro:`Fast JavaScript engines compile web page scripts into native machine code while the page runs, instead of interpreting them line by line. They watch which code runs most and which types it really uses, then generate specialised fast code for those paths. JavaScript becomes quick enough for full applications, such as mail, maps and office tools, inside a browser.`,how:`V8 compiles functions straight to machine code and uses hidden classes: objects with the same shape share a description, so looking up a property becomes reading a fixed memory offset. Inline caches remember what happened at each call site and skip the lookup next time. A generational garbage collector clears short-lived objects quickly, keeping pauses short. Other engines trace hot loops and compile just those.`,history:`In 2006 Lars Bak, a Danish engineer who had built fast virtual machines for Smalltalk and Java, began V8 for Google in Aarhus, Denmark. In June 2008 Apple announced SquirrelFish, and in August 2008 Mozilla announced TraceMonkey, each promising big speed-ups. In September 2008 Google released its Chrome browser with V8. In 2009 Ryan Dahl built Node.js on V8 to run JavaScript on servers. In 2017 all major browsers shipped WebAssembly, a compact binary format that the same engines compile to fast code.`,facts:[`Chrome launched in 2008 with a comic by Scott McCloud explaining how V8 worked.`,`Node.js, built on V8 in 2009, brought JavaScript to web servers.`]},"tech:devops":{intro:`DevOps joins the people who write software and the people who run it into one team with shared goals. Continuous delivery means every change passes through an automated pipeline of builds, tests and deployments, so new versions can reach customers many times a day at the push of a button. Releases become small, routine and easy to undo, instead of rare and risky.`,how:`Servers are configured by code kept in version control, so environments can be rebuilt identically. A pipeline builds each change, runs tests at several levels and deploys to a staging system, then to production, often to a small share of users first. Dashboards and alerts watch the live system, and the developers who made a change are on call when it fails, which pushes them to build software that is easy to run.`,history:`In June 2009 John Allspaw and Paul Hammond of Flickr told the Velocity conference how their developers and operations staff worked together to deploy many times a day. In October 2009 Patrick Debois organised the first DevOpsDays in Ghent, Belgium, and the short name stuck. In 2010 Jez Humble and David Farley published the book Continuous Delivery. In 2011 Amazon said it was deploying new code every few seconds on average. In 2013 Gene Kim's novel The Phoenix Project told the story of a failing IT department saved by DevOps. In 2018 Nicole Forsgren, Jez Humble and Gene Kim's book Accelerate showed with survey data that teams deploying most often also had the fewest failures.`,facts:[`Amazon reported in 2011 that it deployed code every 11.6 seconds on average.`,`The first DevOpsDays conference was held in Ghent in 2009.`]},"tech:golang":{intro:`Cloud-native languages are designed for writing network servers that run on many-core machines in data centres. Go, the leading example, is deliberately small and plain, compiles very fast to a single self-contained program, manages memory with a garbage collector, and makes it easy to run thousands of lightweight tasks at once and pass messages between them.`,how:`A goroutine is a function started with the word go; the runtime spreads many thousands of them over a few operating system threads. Channels pass values safely between goroutines, so tasks share by communicating rather than by locking shared memory. Interfaces are satisfied implicitly, with no class hierarchy, and the standard tools format, test and build code the same way for everyone.`,history:`In September 2007 Robert Griesemer, Rob Pike and Ken Thompson at Google began sketching a new language, frustrated by the long build times of their C++ programs. In November 2009 Google released Go as open source. In March 2012 Go 1 came with a promise that programs written for it would keep compiling with future versions. In 2015 Go 1.5 rewrote the compiler in Go itself and cut garbage-collection pauses sharply. In March 2022 Go 1.18 added generics, after more than a decade of debate.`,facts:[`Ken Thompson, co-creator of Go in 2009, had co-created Unix forty years earlier.`,`Go 1.18 added generics in 2022.`]},"tech:pkgregistry":{intro:`A package registry is a public online library of reusable code, with a command-line tool that fetches any package by name, together with every package it depends on, at the right versions. Publishing a library to the world takes one command. Programmers build on thousands of small shared pieces instead of writing everything themselves.`,how:`Each package declares its name, version and dependencies in a manifest file. The tool works out a set of versions that satisfies every requirement, downloads them from the registry and records the exact choices in a lock file, so the build can be repeated. Semantic versioning, in which a change to the first number warns of breaking changes, lets tools pick safe upgrades automatically.`,history:`In 1995 CPAN, the Comprehensive Perl Archive Network, began collecting Perl modules on mirror sites around the world. In January 2010 Isaac Schlueter released npm for the new Node.js. From 2010 npm let each package carry its own copies of its dependencies, which avoided version clashes and encouraged very small packages. In March 2016 a developer removed his tiny left-pad package from npm, breaking the builds of thousands of projects, including Facebook's React. In 2019 npm passed one million packages, the largest registry of any language. In 2020 GitHub bought npm Inc.`,facts:[`The left-pad package whose removal broke builds in 2016 was 11 lines long.`,`npm passed one million packages in 2019.`]},"tech:royaltyfree":{intro:`Royalty-free video codecs are compression formats whose owners promise not to charge patent fees, so any browser, phone or website can use them without a licence. They compete with patent-pooled standards on quality while letting open-source software such as Firefox play video natively, without plug-ins or fees.`,how:`VP8 works much like H.264: blocks predicted from earlier frames with motion vectors, a transform, quantisation and an arithmetic-style entropy coder. Its patents are licensed to everyone free, with a clause that ends the licence for anyone who sues over them. The WebM container packs VP8 video with Vorbis audio for the web, so a page's video tag can play it directly.`,history:`In 2001 On2 Technologies released its VP3 codec as open source, and in 2004 it became the basis of the free Theora format. In 2009 browser makers argued over which codec the new HTML5 video tag should use: Apple backed patent-pooled H.264, while Mozilla and Opera backed Theora. In May 2010 Google released VP8 as part of WebM at its I/O conference, with Mozilla and Opera on board. In March 2013 MPEG LA agreed to drop plans for a VP8 patent pool after a deal with Google. In 2013 VP9 followed, roughly halving VP8's bit rate, and YouTube adopted it.`,facts:[`Theora, the earlier free codec, grew in 2004 from On2's VP3.`,`MPEG LA dropped its planned VP8 patent pool after a deal with Google in 2013.`]},"tech:spark":{intro:`In-memory analytics engines run large data jobs across a cluster while keeping the working data in the machines' memory instead of writing it to disk after every step. Repeated steps, such as the passes of a machine-learning algorithm, and interactive questions become many times faster. Programmers write short chains of operations in a few lines of Scala instead of rigid map and reduce jobs.`,how:`Spark's core is the resilient distributed dataset: a collection split into partitions across machines, built by operations such as map, filter and join. Rather than copying data for safety, Spark remembers the chain of operations that produced each partition, so a lost one can be recomputed. Work is lazy: nothing runs until a result is needed, which lets the engine plan the whole job at once.`,history:`In 2009 Matei Zaharia, a PhD student at the University of California, Berkeley, began Spark to speed up machine-learning jobs that ran slowly on Hadoop. In June 2010 the team released it as open source with a paper at the HotCloud workshop. In 2013 the project moved to the Apache Software Foundation, and its creators founded Databricks. In May 2014 Spark 1.0 was released. In 2014 Spark set a record by sorting 100 terabytes in 23 minutes, three times faster than the previous Hadoop record with a tenth of the machines.`,facts:[`In 2014 Spark sorted 100 TB in 23 minutes using 206 machines.`,`Zaharia's 2010 paper reported speed-ups of about 10 times over Hadoop on repeated jobs.`]},"tech:typedjs":{intro:`Typed JavaScript adds optional type annotations to JavaScript, such as saying a function takes a string and returns a number, and checks them before the program runs. The types are then stripped away, leaving plain JavaScript that runs in any browser. Editors use the types to catch mistakes as you type, suggest completions and safely rename things across a large code base.`,how:`TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript: every JavaScript program is already valid, so teams can add types gradually. Its type system is structural, meaning two types match if they have the same shape, which fits how JavaScript objects are really used. Declaration files describe the types of existing libraries, and a language service feeds errors and completions to editors.`,history:`In 2009 Google's Closure Compiler checked types written in special comments for its large JavaScript programs, such as Gmail. In October 2011 Google announced Dart, a new language meant to replace JavaScript. In October 2012 Microsoft released TypeScript, designed by Anders Hejlsberg, creator of Turbo Pascal and C#. In November 2014 Facebook released Flow, a rival type checker. In September 2016 Google's Angular 2 framework, written in TypeScript, brought many web developers to it.`,facts:[`TypeScript's designer, Anders Hejlsberg, had written the compiler behind Turbo Pascal in 1983.`,`Angular 2, released in 2016, was written in TypeScript.`]},"tech:cloudwarehouse":{intro:`A cloud data warehouse is an analytics database rented as a service: the provider runs the machines, storage grows without limit, and customers pay for the data stored and the computing their queries use. A company can load billions of rows and query them in seconds without buying servers or hiring administrators, and scale up for a busy month.`,how:`Data sits in compressed column files in the provider's cheap, durable storage. When a query arrives, the service puts as many computers as it needs to work, each reading only the columns and file sections the question touches, and combines their partial answers. When storage and computing are kept separate, several teams can query the same data at once on their own clusters.`,history:`In 2010 Google announced BigQuery, a public version of its internal Dremel query engine. In November 2012 Amazon announced Redshift, based on technology licensed from ParAccel. In 2012 Benoit Dageville, Thierry Cruanes and Marcin Zukowski founded Snowflake, designed from the start to separate storage from computing. In 2015 Snowflake opened its service to all customers. In September 2020 Snowflake's stock market debut was the largest ever for a software company.`,facts:[`Amazon priced Redshift at under $1,000 per terabyte per year in 2012.`,`Snowflake's 2020 flotation raised about $3.4 billion.`]},"tech:docker":{intro:`Container images package an application together with everything it needs, libraries, settings and system tools, into one bundle that runs the same on a developer's laptop, a test server or the cloud. A short recipe file builds the image, and a public registry lets anyone download ready-made images for databases, web servers and languages with one command.`,how:`A Dockerfile lists steps such as: start from an Ubuntu image, copy in the code, install packages. Each step makes a read-only layer, and layers are shared between images, so only the changes are stored or downloaded. When the image runs, its layers are stacked under a thin writable layer and the program starts inside a Linux container with its own namespaces and resource limits.`,history:`In 2008 Solomon Hykes and friends founded dotCloud in Paris to host other people's web applications, using Linux containers behind the scenes. In March 2013 Hykes showed Docker to the public in a five-minute lightning talk at the PyCon conference in Santa Clara. In October 2013 dotCloud renamed itself Docker Inc. In June 2014 Docker 1.0 was released, with backing from Google, Microsoft and Amazon soon after. In June 2015 the Open Container Initiative was formed to standardise image formats. In November 2019 Docker sold its enterprise business to Mirantis.`,facts:[`Hykes's first public demonstration of Docker, at PyCon in 2013, lasted about five minutes.`,`The Open Container Initiative was formed in 2015 to standardise container images.`]},"tech:hevc":{intro:`HEVC, High Efficiency Video Coding, also called H.265, is a video standard that matches the picture quality of H.264 at about half the bit rate. It was built for high-definition and ultra-high-definition 4K screens, where large smooth areas of picture let bigger blocks pay off. Encoding takes far more computing work, but decoding stays affordable for televisions and phones.`,how:`Pictures are divided into coding tree units of up to 64×64 pixels, which split into smaller blocks only where detail needs them. Prediction within a frame offers 35 modes, including 33 angles, and motion prediction merges vectors from neighbouring blocks. Transforms range from 4×4 to 32×32, and the design lets the work be spread across many processor cores.`,history:`In January 2010 the ITU-T and ISO video groups formed the Joint Collaborative Team on Video Coding to build H.264's successor. In April 2013 the ITU-T approved the standard as H.265. In 2014 Netflix began streaming 4K video using HEVC. In 2015 a second patent pool, HEVC Advance, announced fees that at first included a share of content revenue, alarming browser makers and streaming firms. In 2017 Apple adopted HEVC for photos and video on iPhones with iOS 11.`,facts:[`HEVC's largest blocks, 64×64 pixels, cover sixteen times the area of H.264's 16×16 (2013).`,`Apple made HEVC the iPhone's standard video format in 2017.`]},"tech:ans":{intro:`Asymmetric numeral systems are a family of entropy coders, ways of turning symbol probabilities into bits, that compress as tightly as arithmetic coding while running nearly as fast as Huffman coding. The whole state of the coder is a single number, and a table-driven version needs only lookups, shifts and additions for each symbol.`,how:`ANS keeps one whole-number state. Encoding a symbol grows the state by roughly the inverse of the symbol's probability, so likely symbols add less; when the state gets too big, its low bits are written out. Decoding reverses this exactly, recovering symbols in the opposite order, so encoders work backwards through the data. The table-based version precomputes every step; the range-based version uses multiplication instead.`,history:`In 2009 Jarek Duda, of the Jagiellonian University in Kraków, posted his first paper on asymmetric numeral systems. In December 2013 Yann Collet released Finite State Entropy, a fast table-based ANS coder. In 2014 Duda published an expanded description and urged that the method stay free of patents. In 2015 Apple's LZFSE compressor used it. In 2016 Facebook's Zstandard shipped with Collet's coder inside. In 2022 the JPEG XL image format, built on ANS, became an ISO standard.`,facts:[`Facebook's Zstandard, released in 2016, uses ANS through Collet's FSE coder.`,`Duda first described asymmetric numeral systems in 2009.`]},"tech:kubernetes":{intro:`Container orchestration runs containers across a whole cluster of machines automatically. The operator writes down the desired state, such as five copies of this web server and two of that database, and the system decides which machines run them, restarts any that crash, replaces failed machines and adds copies when load rises.`,how:`Kubernetes keeps the desired state in a consistent store called etcd. Control loops compare it with what is actually running and act to close any gap. Containers are grouped into pods that share a network address, and services give a stable name that balances traffic across a changing set of pods. Everything is managed through an API, usually with files written in YAML.`,history:`From about 2004 Google ran nearly all its work on Borg, an internal cluster manager. In June 2014 Craig McLuckie, Joe Beda and Brendan Burns announced Kubernetes, Greek for helmsman, as an open-source system drawing on Borg's lessons. In July 2015 version 1.0 shipped, and Google gave the project to the new Cloud Native Computing Foundation. In 2017 Docker, Microsoft and Amazon all adopted Kubernetes, ending a short war between rival orchestrators. In March 2018 it became the first project to graduate from the foundation.`,facts:[`Kubernetes means helmsman in Greek, and its logo is a seven-spoked ship's wheel (2014).`,`Kubernetes was the first project to graduate from the Cloud Native Computing Foundation, in 2018.`]},"tech:serverless":{intro:`Serverless functions let a developer upload a small piece of code and say what should trigger it, such as a web request, a new file or a message, while the cloud provider handles every server. The code runs only when triggered, scales from zero to thousands of copies automatically, and is billed only for the time it actually runs.`,how:`The provider keeps pools of lightweight sandboxes. When an event arrives, it loads the function into one, runs it and may keep it warm for the next call; the first call after a quiet spell pays a cold-start delay. Functions are expected to be short and stateless, keeping data in cloud databases and storage, so the provider can run as many copies as needed.`,history:`In April 2008 Google App Engine let developers run web applications without managing servers, though in a restricted form. In November 2014 Amazon announced AWS Lambda at its re:Invent conference in Las Vegas. In 2016 Google Cloud Functions, Microsoft's Azure Functions and IBM's OpenWhisk followed. In November 2018 Amazon released Firecracker, the tiny virtual machine monitor that starts Lambda's sandboxes, as open source. In December 2020 Lambda moved to billing by the millisecond instead of in 100-millisecond steps.`,facts:[`Firecracker, released in 2018, starts a micro virtual machine in about 125 milliseconds.`,`From 2020 Lambda billed by the millisecond instead of in 100-millisecond steps.`]},"tech:memsafe":{intro:`Memory-safe systems languages give the speed and low-level control of C and C++ while ruling out a whole class of bugs: reading freed memory, writing past the end of a buffer, and two threads changing the same data at once. The compiler checks these rules before the program runs, with no garbage collector slowing it down.`,how:`In Rust every value has one owner, and it is freed automatically when the owner goes out of scope. Code can borrow a reference, but either many readers or one writer at a time, never both; the borrow checker proves this when compiling. Code that must break the rules, such as talking to hardware, is marked unsafe, so reviewers know exactly where to look.`,history:`In 2006 Graydon Hoare, a programmer at Mozilla, began Rust as a personal project, naming it after a hardy family of fungi. In 2009 Mozilla began sponsoring it, hoping to build a safer browser engine. In May 2015 Rust 1.0 was released with a promise of stability. In November 2017 Firefox shipped Stylo, a style engine written in Rust. In 2019 Microsoft said about 70 per cent of the security bugs it fixed each year were memory-safety problems. In February 2024 the White House urged programmers to move to memory-safe languages.`,facts:[`Microsoft said in 2019 that about 70% of its security bugs were memory-safety bugs.`,`Memory-safety flaws fell from 76% to 35% of Android's vulnerabilities between 2019 and 2022.`]},"tech:lsp":{intro:`A language server is a separate program that understands one programming language deeply, finding definitions, listing completions, showing errors as you type and renaming safely, and offers that knowledge to any editor through a common protocol. Each language needs one server and each editor one client, instead of every editor reimplementing support for every language.`,how:`The editor starts the server and talks to it in JSON messages: this document was opened, this line changed, what is defined at this position. The server keeps its own model of the code, parses and type-checks it in the background, and replies with locations, completion lists and error reports. Turning M editors times N languages of work into M plus N is the whole point.`,history:`In April 2015 Microsoft announced Visual Studio Code, a free editor built on web technology, and made it open source that November. In June 2016 Microsoft, with Red Hat and Codenvy, published the Language Server Protocol, which had grown out of its TypeScript and C# tools. In 2017 Red Hat released a Java language server, bringing full Java support to VS Code and other editors. In 2018 Stack Overflow's survey named VS Code the most popular development environment. By 2020 language servers existed for dozens of languages, with clients for editors from Vim to Emacs.`,facts:[`VS Code was the most popular editor in Stack Overflow's 2018 developer survey.`,`The Language Server Protocol was published in 2016 with Red Hat and Codenvy.`]},"tech:monorepo":{intro:`A monorepo keeps a whole company's code, thousands of projects, in one version-controlled repository. Every team builds from the same head of the tree, so a change to a shared library and fixes to all its users can land in one commit, and nobody juggles incompatible versions. It needs special tools for building and searching at enormous scale.`,how:`A build system such as Google's Blaze, released as Bazel, knows the exact dependencies of every target, so it rebuilds and retests only what a change affects, sharing cached results among all engineers. Virtual file systems show developers the repository without copying it all to their machines. Ownership files say who must review changes to each directory, and automated tools make sweeping edits across millions of files.`,history:`In the early 2010s Google moved its single huge repository from Perforce to Piper, a system it built itself. In January 2014 Facebook described how it had extended Mercurial to cope with its own monorepo. In March 2015 Google released Bazel, the open-source version of its Blaze build tool. In July 2016 Rachel Potvin and Josh Levenberg explained in Communications of the ACM why Google keeps almost all its code in one repository. In 2017 Microsoft moved the code of Windows into one Git repository, using its new Git Virtual File System.`,facts:[`Microsoft's 2017 Windows repository held about 3.5 million files and 300 GB.`,`Google's monorepo took about 40,000 commits on a typical workday in 2016, most from automated tools.`]},"tech:zstd":{intro:`Modern fast compressors rework the old ZIP formula for modern processors: they pack data as tightly as DEFLATE or tighter, many times faster, and decompress faster still. They offer a wide dial of levels, from very fast for live data to very strong for archives, and can use pre-trained dictionaries to shrink small messages.`,how:`Zstandard finds repeats with LZ77-style matching over a large window, then codes literals with Huffman codes and match details with Finite State Entropy, a table-driven ANS coder. Its code is written to keep the processor busy, avoiding unpredictable branches and working on several streams at once. A dictionary trained on sample data lets even tiny records compress well.`,history:`In 2011 Yann Collet, a French programmer, released LZ4, an extremely fast compressor. In 2015 Google released Brotli, and browsers soon adopted it for web pages. In August 2016 Facebook, where Collet had gone to work, released Zstandard 1.0. In November 2017 Linux 4.14 added Zstandard for compressing file systems. In January 2020 Arch Linux switched its packages from xz to Zstandard. In March 2024 Google Chrome began accepting web pages compressed with it.`,facts:[`Arch Linux found in 2020 that Zstandard unpacked its packages about 13 times faster than xz.`,`Zstandard became an internet standard, RFC 8478, in 2018.`]},"tech:av1":{intro:`AV1 is an open, royalty-free video format created by a group of web, chip and streaming companies. It aims to beat HEVC's compression without any patent fees, so it can be built into every browser, phone and television. Its members pledge their patents free to all users and promise to defend the format together.`,how:`AV1 merges ideas from Google's VP9, Mozilla's Daala and Cisco's Thor. Blocks range up to 128×128 pixels in many shapes; extra tools predict colour from brightness, copy earlier patterns within a frame for screen content, and add synthetic film grain back at the decoder rather than spending bits on noise. Encoding is very slow at first, so hardware support matters.`,history:`In September 2015 Amazon, Cisco, Google, Intel, Microsoft, Mozilla and Netflix founded the Alliance for Open Media. In January 2018 Apple joined the alliance. In March 2018 the AV1 format was frozen as version 1.0. In 2019 the licensing firm Sisvel formed a patent pool claiming AV1 needed licences, which the alliance rejected. In February 2020 Netflix began streaming AV1 to Android phones. In September 2023 Apple's iPhone 15 Pro added hardware AV1 decoding.`,facts:[`Seven companies founded the Alliance for Open Media in 2015.`,`Apple joined the Alliance for Open Media in 2018.`]},"tech:lakehouse":{intro:`A lakehouse keeps all of an organisation's data as open-format files in cheap cloud storage, like a data lake, but adds a table layer that gives it database features: transactions, schemas, versions and fast SQL. The same copy of the data serves reporting, data science and machine learning, without loading it into a separate warehouse.`,how:`Tables are folders of column-oriented Parquet files plus a transaction log or metadata tree that records exactly which files make up each version of the table. Writers add files and commit a new log entry in one step, so readers always see a consistent snapshot and can even query the table as it was last week. Any engine that understands the open format can read the same table.`,history:`In 2016 Uber built Hudi to update huge tables of files in place. In 2017 Ryan Blue and Dan Weeks at Netflix began Iceberg, a table format for its huge data lake, and gave it to Apache in 2018. In April 2019 Databricks released Delta Lake as open source. In January 2020 Databricks popularised the word lakehouse in a blog post, and in 2021 its founders argued the case in a paper at the CIDR conference. In June 2024 Databricks bought Tabular, the company founded by Iceberg's creators.`,facts:[`Databricks paid over $1 billion for Tabular, founded by Iceberg's creators, in 2024.`,`Uber built Apache Hudi, another open table format, in 2016.`]},"tech:supplychain":{intro:`Software supply-chain security protects everything that goes into a program before it reaches users: open-source packages, build servers, compilers and update channels. It means knowing every component a product contains, proving that each was built from the expected source by a trusted system, and checking signatures so nobody can slip in a tampered piece along the way.`,how:`A software bill of materials lists every component and version in a product, so a newly found flaw can be traced to every affected system. Builds run on locked-down, repeatable systems that record signed provenance: which source, which steps, which machine. Tools such as Sigstore let developers sign releases with short-lived certificates tied to their identity and log every signature publicly, so tampering can be spotted.`,history:`In December 2020 investigators found that attackers had hidden a backdoor in updates of SolarWinds' Orion network software, reaching thousands of customers including US government agencies. In 2021 Google proposed SLSA, a ladder of levels for trustworthy builds, and the Sigstore signing service launched under the Linux Foundation. In May 2021 a US presidential executive order called for software sold to the government to come with a bill of materials. In December 2021 the Log4Shell flaw in Log4j, a Java logging library hidden inside countless products, showed how deep dependencies run. In March 2024 a backdoor planted over two years in the xz compression tools was caught weeks before it reached the stable versions of major Linux distributions.`,facts:[`The SolarWinds backdoor of 2020 reached about 18,000 customers through routine updates.`,`Log4Shell, found in 2021, was rated 10 out of 10 for severity.`]},"tech:neuralcodec":{intro:`Neural compression uses trained neural networks to compress data. A model that predicts the next piece of text, sound or picture well can feed its guesses to an arithmetic or ANS coder, shrinking data far beyond classic tools, while other networks learn to turn pictures and sound into compact codes. The price is enormous computation, and both sides must hold the same model.`,how:`Any predictor can become a compressor: for each next symbol the model gives a probability for every possibility, and an entropy coder spends few bits on symbols it rated likely and many on surprises. Better prediction means fewer bits. Learned codecs for audio and images use an encoder network that turns the signal into a small set of numbers, which are rounded and stored, and a decoder network that rebuilds the signal.`,history:`In 2006 Marcus Hutter launched a prize for compressing a 100 MB extract of Wikipedia, arguing that good compression requires understanding. In 2019 Fabrice Bellard's NNCP used a neural network to reach the top of text-compression benchmarks. In 2020 Hutter enlarged the prize and the test file tenfold. In 2021 Google's SoundStream showed a neural audio codec beating classic codecs at very low bit rates. In September 2023 a DeepMind paper, 'Language Modeling Is Compression', showed a large language model out-compressing PNG on images and FLAC on audio, despite being trained only on text.`,facts:[`The Hutter Prize grew to 500,000 euros in 2020, for compressing 1 GB of Wikipedia.`,`Google's SoundStream codec of 2021 carried speech at just 3 kilobits a second.`]}};export{e as PEDIA_SOFTWARE};